So with season one, it was
like, we were doing best-evers.

00:10:55.070 --> 00:10:58.210
We also went to Texas, and
we shot a couple of episodes.

00:10:58.210 --> 00:10:59.560
You talk about talking quietly,

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:02.320
we did like 20 worst tattoos ever,

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:04.680
which that video performed really well.

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:06.410
We did it inside of a tattoo.

00:11:06.410 --> 00:11:08.290
Yeah, when we were making the...

00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:12.190
'cause we were still
making commercials for

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:14.700
local businesses still.

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:19.010
We went after Commercial Kings,

00:11:19.010 --> 00:11:20.770
we went back to doing that.

00:11:20.770 --> 00:11:22.780
What episode was that, what number?

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:25.000
108, "20 Worst Tattoos Ever."

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:26.320
We were both talking quietly,

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:28.272
'cause we were self-conscious.

00:11:28.272 --> 00:11:31.100
Yeah, if you put me in a situation

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:33.510
where I feel like I might
be offending someone,

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:35.180
or making someone else feel uncomfortable,

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:37.100
that's when my personality changes.

00:11:37.100 --> 00:11:40.520
And then we did take a
break, to our credit.

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:44.290
We took a summer break,
even after season one,

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:47.480
and we did that thing called
"Rhett and Link Vault,"

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:49.453
where we were like, "Hey,
we got some old videos.

00:11:49.453 --> 00:11:50.930
Maybe you haven't seen them,

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:53.250
we're gonna contextualize them,

00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:55.880
and then you're supposed to rewatch them."

00:11:55.880 --> 00:11:58.550
We were thinking about taking breaks,

00:11:58.550 --> 00:12:02.229
but still keeping content
coming over the summer.

00:12:02.229 --> 00:12:05.637
I mean, that was a smart move to say,

00:12:05.637 --> 00:12:09.120
"This is not gonna be
every single day forever,"

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:09.953
you know?

00:12:09.953 --> 00:12:10.890
As we talked to other creators now,

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:12.432
we still very much say,

00:12:12.432 --> 00:12:15.840
if there's a way for you to
set up how you work on things

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:19.490
so that you can look forward
to your own creative breaks

00:12:19.490 --> 00:12:22.800
so you're not obligated
to keep churning stuff out

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:26.293
with no breaks, that's a smart move.

00:12:26.293 --> 00:12:27.920
I'm glad that we did that.
And also,

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:30.723
at the time, if I'm
remembering this correctly,

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:35.550
we had not yet developed any sort of

00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:38.390
block shooting schedule, which, you know,-

00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:39.223
Oh, hell no.

00:12:39.223 --> 00:12:41.030
We've revealed the secret about that,

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:42.990
about how we don't come in
and shoot every single day.

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:45.310
But at that time we were,

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:48.410
so you were coming in and
shooting every single day

00:12:48.410 --> 00:12:51.100
at season one, so if
you didn't take a break,

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:52.801
there was no vacation.

00:12:52.801 --> 00:12:55.150
There was nothing, you know?

00:12:55.150 --> 00:12:57.112
And I definitely remember

00:12:57.112 --> 00:12:58.930
there were times when it's like,

00:12:58.930 --> 00:13:01.760
and we would sit down to do an episode,

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:02.593
and they were...

00:13:03.638 --> 00:13:08.060
The concepts were very
loose, and it was like,

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:09.900
okay, I assume you're
gonna bring something,

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:11.450
I'm gonna bring something,

00:13:11.450 --> 00:13:13.000
and we're gonna try to make this thing

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:16.030
land around 10 minutes, and
we're not gonna edit it.

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:19.140
So there's a push and
pull of creative tension

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:21.281
between the two of us of, okay,

00:13:21.281 --> 00:13:23.720
I thought you were taken
over at this point,

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:25.100
I thought you were
bringing this perspective.

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:26.230
We had to work through that stuff-

00:13:26.230 --> 00:13:29.340
And there's no saving
it with another camera,

00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:30.820
or a jump cut.

00:13:30.820 --> 00:13:35.180
I don't know when the first
jump cut in GMM history was

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:37.280
the first edit where something was hidden.

00:13:38.165 --> 00:13:41.910
It didn't happen much, if
any, in the first season.

00:13:41.910 --> 00:13:43.490
And I remember there were
times when it was like,

00:13:43.490 --> 00:13:44.323
you know what?

00:13:44.323 --> 00:13:45.713
I don't feel like doing this.

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:48.950
Let's just take a walk,

00:13:48.950 --> 00:13:50.090
or let's not do it.

00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:50.923
Let's get some lunch.

00:13:50.923 --> 00:13:53.300
We'll shoot two in a row tomorrow.

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:54.210
Yeah, we never lost-

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:56.853
Or we would start it, and like,

00:13:57.960 --> 00:13:59.280
one of us would be like, you know what?

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:02.040
We'd stop like five minutes
in and be like, yeah,

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:03.660
we're not to the thing that we said

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:05.840
we were actually gonna talk
about, let's start over,

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:06.673
Yeah, every once in a while.

00:14:06.673 --> 00:14:08.670
And then they'd be this risk of it,

00:14:08.670 --> 00:14:11.730
the other person reacting as
if they were being critiqued.

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:16.070
And the dynamic there,
it was touchy, right?

00:14:16.070 --> 00:14:18.850
'Cause it was like, you
both have to bring your best

00:14:18.850 --> 00:14:21.650
and feel confident, and you
got to feed off each other.

00:14:21.650 --> 00:14:24.008
And if anything goes a little wrong,

00:14:24.008 --> 00:14:26.850
I mean, we avoided as much as we could,

00:14:26.850 --> 00:14:28.150
but there were definitely
times where it's like,

00:14:28.150 --> 00:14:29.339
okay, abandon ship.

00:14:29.339 --> 00:14:33.120
It's like, I can tell that
you're getting frustrated with me

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:35.530
because I've done something,

00:14:35.530 --> 00:14:37.420
said something that just
rubbed you the wrong way,

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:39.690
or vice versa, you know?

00:14:39.690 --> 00:14:40.990
I remember that.

00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:43.200
It would happen, but it was pretty rare.

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:45.030
Well, Jason was there.

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:47.253
Without a third person there,

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:50.230
like, we would have been sunk,

00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:53.210
because I think those moments
would have happened even more.

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:56.314
It's like, you tend to be
on a little better behavior.

00:14:56.314 --> 00:14:59.240
But we were committed to the concept

00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:01.040
of continuing the show.

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:02.010
Oh yeah.

00:15:02.010 --> 00:15:03.080
So we would have figured it out,

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:06.035
but the other thing that we were doing

00:15:06.035 --> 00:15:10.190
that I feel like is, you know...

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:14.240
what has gotten us to
this point in our career

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:18.220
is just this combination
of good fortune and timing,

00:15:18.220 --> 00:15:22.310
and great help and some good instincts.

00:15:22.310 --> 00:15:24.990
But one of the things
that we did as a choice

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:26.918
that I'm very happy that we made,

00:15:26.918 --> 00:15:31.110
is that even though we didn't
have a production schedule,

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:32.654
per se,

00:15:32.654 --> 00:15:36.855
what most of our peers
were doing at the time

00:15:36.855 --> 00:15:41.855
is they didn't have regularly
scheduled programming, right?

00:15:42.604 --> 00:15:43.983
So what happens is,

00:15:43.983 --> 00:15:46.750
because you don't always
feel like making things.

00:15:46.750 --> 00:15:48.410
And so when you're talking
about this scenario,

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:49.840
if we were doing this hand to mouth thing

00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:51.530
where we were coming
in and we were filming,

00:15:51.530 --> 00:15:53.210
and then we were posting when it was done,

00:15:53.210 --> 00:15:56.370
which is pretty much what
everybody was doing at the time,

00:15:56.370 --> 00:15:59.030
and pretty much what everybody
still does in a lot of ways.

00:15:59.030 --> 00:15:59.863
Yeah.

00:15:59.863 --> 00:16:00.696
A lot of people still do it.

00:16:00.696 --> 00:16:01.690
It gives you this out.

00:16:01.690 --> 00:16:02.733
Well, and then you're just like-

00:16:02.733 --> 00:16:04.440
There's a fear of like,
if I commit to something,

00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:05.470
and then-
But not only that,

00:16:05.470 --> 00:16:06.710
if you don't feel like doing it,

00:16:06.710 --> 00:16:08.410
you say, "Okay, guys,"

00:16:08.410 --> 00:16:09.337
you go on Twitter and you say,

00:16:09.337 --> 00:16:10.650
"Guys, the video is gonna be late,"

00:16:10.650 --> 00:16:13.760
or, "So sorry, no video this week."

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:18.250
We've never done that in 10
years, that I can remember.

00:16:18.250 --> 00:16:19.240
Yeah, that's right.

00:16:19.240 --> 00:16:20.310
It's like, the season,

00:16:20.310 --> 00:16:23.190
this is when the season is
is Monday through Friday.

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:25.550
There's been a couple of
times where an episode

00:16:25.550 --> 00:16:28.040
either got pulled down for
some reason, or whatever,

00:16:28.040 --> 00:16:31.060
and then we made another,
and put it on the society.

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:33.510
Or I actually saw early on in season one,

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:35.700
like a few episodes in there was that,

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:36.810
because of what was happening

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:39.030
with some internet legislation,
we did like a blackout,

00:16:39.030 --> 00:16:40.560
and we didn't do an episode that day.

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:41.420
Yeah.

00:16:41.420 --> 00:16:45.060
But I think because we didn't
give ourselves that out,

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:46.750
that's one of the reasons
that, 10 years later,

00:16:46.750 --> 00:16:49.910
we're looking back at 21 seasons.

00:16:49.910 --> 00:16:52.440
Yeah, season two, August of 2012,

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:55.430
that's when we introduced Time Rangerers.

00:16:55.430 --> 00:16:57.098
I mean, we still have the puppets

00:16:57.098 --> 00:17:00.120
sent by mythical beast in our likenesses,

00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:05.120
and then, so that would
be within an episode,

00:17:05.140 --> 00:17:06.400
but it wouldn't be,

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:09.370
we talk about something for a
little bit, and then we would-

00:17:09.370 --> 00:17:11.230
But we put it in the thumbnail.

00:17:11.230 --> 00:17:13.670
And there was no...

00:17:13.670 --> 00:17:16.760
the scripting was
extremely loose for that,

00:17:16.760 --> 00:17:17.910
and I just remember the,

00:17:17.910 --> 00:17:19.650
you remember the catchphrase?

00:17:19.650 --> 00:17:20.673
Give me a second.

00:17:22.580 --> 00:17:24.235
I didn't watch any of these videos,

00:17:24.235 --> 00:17:26.655
this has just come back to me

00:17:26.655 --> 00:17:28.237
We would say it together.

00:17:28.237 --> 00:17:31.030
Yeah, we were horrible at puppetaring.

00:17:31.030 --> 00:17:33.160
It was our version of like
to infinity and beyond,

00:17:33.160 --> 00:17:34.127
or something.

00:17:34.127 --> 00:17:35.940
To the time machine, it was like,

00:17:35.940 --> 00:17:37.240
something about the time machine.

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:38.250
To the gazebo.

00:17:38.250 --> 00:17:39.706
To the gazebo.
The gazebo was

00:17:39.706 --> 00:17:40.808
the time machine.
The gazebo was

00:17:40.808 --> 00:17:41.970
the time machine.

00:17:41.970 --> 00:17:43.110
And I think Time Rangerers-
To the gazebo.

00:17:43.110 --> 00:17:47.060
Might have just been a
mispronunciation of Time Rangers

00:17:47.060 --> 00:17:48.910
in the first episode, I don't know.

00:17:48.910 --> 00:17:50.550
Well, based on the thumbnails,

00:17:50.550 --> 00:17:53.263
and the associated view counts,

00:17:54.160 --> 00:17:56.400
puppets, not a strong choice.

00:17:56.400 --> 00:17:58.490
We did "Best Ever Fridays."

00:17:58.490 --> 00:18:00.960
Wow, people hate puppets.

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:02.301
People hate puppets.

00:18:02.301 --> 00:18:06.097
Episode 37, "How to Make
Someone Sound Like an Idiot."

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:11.470
So this was an early
important episode for us,

00:18:11.470 --> 00:18:15.544
because we talked about the Darwin Awards,

00:18:15.544 --> 00:18:20.010
and so we talked about the thing that won,

00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:21.710
and then we talked about another thing,

00:18:21.710 --> 00:18:25.834
and then we talked about
the speech jammer device

00:18:25.834 --> 00:18:28.360
that you could point it at
somebody, it's in the thumbnail,

00:18:28.360 --> 00:18:30.710
but like we said, "Hey,
we can do this ourselves

00:18:30.710 --> 00:18:34.240
using the delay within 'Garage Band.'"

00:18:34.240 --> 00:18:37.250
And you listen to yourself
talking on a delay,

00:18:37.250 --> 00:18:38.850
and you sound like an idiot,

00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:41.220
and that was absolutely hilarious.

00:18:41.220 --> 00:18:43.440
But we waited until, I don't know,

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:44.660
seven, eight minutes

00:18:44.660 --> 00:18:46.380
into the episode.
When the episode was over.

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:48.250
Before we actually began demonstrating it,

00:18:48.250 --> 00:18:49.840
because the first half of the episode

00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:54.100
we were talking about
those inventions, right?

00:18:54.100 --> 00:18:57.257
And like, the research side of things.

00:18:57.257 --> 00:19:01.390
And we've told this story
at some point, I'm sure,

00:19:01.390 --> 00:19:06.214
but a good friend and manager
of ours at the time was,

00:19:06.214 --> 00:19:08.943
we were talking about that episode,

00:19:08.943 --> 00:19:10.610
and he was like, "Hey,"

00:19:10.610 --> 00:19:13.830
and this was actually like
a year after the fact.

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:15.280
Yeah, this was Dan.

00:19:15.280 --> 00:19:17.380
We were talking about kind of making some,

00:19:17.380 --> 00:19:19.640
like, we were evaluating GMM.

00:19:19.640 --> 00:19:20.737
And he was like, you know.

00:19:20.737 --> 00:19:23.100
Yes, like we said we
wanted to put sponsors in,

00:19:23.100 --> 00:19:25.860
we had a slot for sponsors
from the beginning,

00:19:25.860 --> 00:19:27.087
but we were like,

00:19:27.087 --> 00:19:29.340
"Well, we need to be making
more money doing this thing."

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:31.040
So like, that's why we were having

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:32.920
these conversations with Dan.

00:19:32.920 --> 00:19:34.800
And he said, "You know,
my favorite episode

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:37.010
is the speech jammer episode,

00:19:37.010 --> 00:19:38.460
and I sent it to people,

00:19:38.460 --> 00:19:40.640
but I always have to,
when I send it to people,

00:19:40.640 --> 00:19:44.540
I say, 'Fast forward to
like seven or eight minutes

00:19:44.540 --> 00:19:45.700
in when I started doing this.'"

00:19:45.700 --> 00:19:46.533
Right.

00:19:46.533 --> 00:19:48.030
And then it was like, well,

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:51.910
why don't we make the
episode about one thing?

00:19:51.910 --> 00:19:54.020
Which was typically what we did,

00:19:54.020 --> 00:19:56.780
but it was sort of a re-evaluation
of what we were doing.

00:19:56.780 --> 00:19:58.650
It's like, if people are clicking on this

00:19:58.650 --> 00:20:01.110
because of this expectation,

00:20:01.110 --> 00:20:02.980
we want to deliver on that expectation,

00:20:02.980 --> 00:20:07.124
and that was a pretty
significant mind shift for us.

00:20:07.124 --> 00:20:10.420
Yeah, and I think there was
a little bit of fear in it,

00:20:10.420 --> 00:20:12.010
'cause we would plan an
episode, we'd be like,

00:20:12.010 --> 00:20:14.410
we gotta make sure that
we can fill the time.

00:20:14.410 --> 00:20:17.590
What if, you know, in real time?

00:20:17.590 --> 00:20:19.160
So we're gonna save
this thing that we know

00:20:19.160 --> 00:20:20.870
is gonna be funny for a little bit later,

00:20:20.870 --> 00:20:23.270
so that the episode is full.

00:20:23.270 --> 00:20:25.130
We didn't have enough
confidence that like, okay,

00:20:25.130 --> 00:20:27.430
you take one thing that
people want to click on,

00:20:27.430 --> 00:20:29.190
and then you flesh it out.

00:20:29.190 --> 00:20:32.920
It's like, no we've got to
throw other things in first

00:20:32.920 --> 00:20:36.400
to make sure that we can break 10 minutes.

00:20:36.400 --> 00:20:38.820
And once we made that shift and said,

00:20:38.820 --> 00:20:40.270
no, you have a good concept,

00:20:40.270 --> 00:20:42.170
you lead with it and you flesh it out,

00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:47.070
that certainly helped
the product drastically.

00:20:47.070 --> 00:20:48.430
I mean, one of the things I'm noticing

00:20:48.430 --> 00:20:50.920
just looking at these old thumbnails

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:54.643
and the associated views is

00:20:54.643 --> 00:20:59.420
there's an incredible
range of how low it can be

00:20:59.420 --> 00:21:00.736
and how high it can be, right?

00:21:00.736 --> 00:21:04.040
There's a psychopath smell test

00:21:04.040 --> 00:21:05.630
where we're wearing blindfolds.

00:21:05.630 --> 00:21:06.770
Episode 38.

00:21:06.770 --> 00:21:09.790
Which has got 5.8 million
views from nine years ago.

00:21:09.790 --> 00:21:13.700
And then there's the puppet
episode right next to it.

00:21:13.700 --> 00:21:14.533
Well, over the years

00:21:14.533 --> 00:21:15.667
people have gone back.
Right.

00:21:15.667 --> 00:21:18.650
And cherry picked ones
that I think resonate more

00:21:18.650 --> 00:21:20.400
with where the show ended up going.

00:21:21.503 --> 00:21:24.320
I feel like I can actually
look at these thumbnails

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:25.838
without view counts,

00:21:25.838 --> 00:21:29.300
and be pretty accurately predict
which ones performed well

00:21:29.300 --> 00:21:31.760
now that 10 years of all this data

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:33.327
of how people respond to
titles and thumbnails.

00:21:33.327 --> 00:21:35.830
it was just so experimental at the time.

00:21:35.830 --> 00:21:37.616
And the feedback,

00:21:37.616 --> 00:21:40.210
we're looking at 10 years
of collective feedback

00:21:40.210 --> 00:21:42.510
on these videos, 9 or 10 years.

00:21:42.510 --> 00:21:43.343
Yeah.

00:21:43.343 --> 00:21:45.646
And so you see this
cumulative effect of like,

00:21:45.646 --> 00:21:49.133
no one cares about
gladiators versus puppets

00:21:49.133 --> 00:21:54.133
to the tune of 273,000 views
next to a 6 million view video.

00:21:54.780 --> 00:21:59.010
And then we wrapped up
season two with episode 86,

00:21:59.010 --> 00:22:01.590
which was a Christmas special, so-

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:02.423
Oh, yeah.

00:22:02.423 --> 00:22:05.608
You know, even though it was
the first year of the show,

00:22:05.608 --> 00:22:10.530
it was still a side
project in a lot of ways.

00:22:10.530 --> 00:22:12.400
It was like, let's do something,

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:14.600
I mean, we're gonna take a holiday break,

00:22:14.600 --> 00:22:15.550
let's do a special.

00:22:15.550 --> 00:22:17.360
And we threw all this stuff at it,

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:18.988
Julian Smith was in that.

00:22:18.988 --> 00:22:23.920
Yeah, 575,000 views, which
is pretty low for that time.

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:25.021
Which, again.

00:22:25.021 --> 00:22:26.740
It was a lot of work.

00:22:26.740 --> 00:22:28.513
How long is it?
28 minutes.

00:22:29.830 --> 00:22:30.663
But-

00:22:30.663 --> 00:22:31.496
It was special.

00:22:31.496 --> 00:22:35.733
I think that the decision
to make that special,

00:22:37.110 --> 00:22:38.310
and make it 28 minutes,

00:22:38.310 --> 00:22:40.350
and make it a variety show

00:22:40.350 --> 00:22:42.317
was what led directly to

00:22:42.317 --> 00:22:43.930
"The Mythical Show".
"The Mythical Show".

00:22:43.930 --> 00:22:45.380
You're exactly right.

00:22:45.380 --> 00:22:47.470
That's why we decided to
do "The Mythical Show".

00:22:47.470 --> 00:22:50.150
Until we were on our
own, we had done this,

00:22:50.150 --> 00:22:50.983
and it was like, well,

00:22:50.983 --> 00:22:55.373
if we can do it, we can do it
again, and people liked it.

00:22:57.079 --> 00:22:59.713
I'm gonna put my phone on non-buzz.

00:23:00.570 --> 00:23:02.150
Let's do an ad.

00:23:02.150 --> 00:23:03.450
Non-buzz.

00:23:03.450 --> 00:23:06.050
You know something else people like?

00:23:06.050 --> 00:23:07.770
Getting stuff from our store.

00:23:07.770 --> 00:23:09.130
We really appreciate that.

00:23:09.130 --> 00:23:12.670
We have a lot of stuff over
at mythical.com for sale.

00:23:12.670 --> 00:23:13.711
But it's a rotating

00:23:13.711 --> 00:23:14.970
menagerie.
But it gets gone.

00:23:14.970 --> 00:23:16.670
I mean, people like it,

00:23:16.670 --> 00:23:18.390
they like the stuff that's happening.

00:23:18.390 --> 00:23:21.080
Our team is creating
incredible merchandise,

00:23:21.080 --> 00:23:22.100
and people are buying it.

00:23:22.100 --> 00:23:23.460
It goes away.

00:23:23.460 --> 00:23:25.150
And also, we like to keep it fresh.

00:23:25.150 --> 00:23:28.130
I mean, this isn't like
your grandma's house

00:23:28.130 --> 00:23:28.990
where you're gonna show up,

00:23:28.990 --> 00:23:31.060
and she's gonna make exactly the same,

00:23:31.060 --> 00:23:33.615
fried chicken or country-style steak.

00:23:33.615 --> 00:23:35.690
We're not your grandma, right? Okay?

00:23:35.690 --> 00:23:37.150
We've got things we don't change,

00:23:37.150 --> 00:23:39.460
but the thing we do change
is we change the merch.

00:23:39.460 --> 00:23:40.420
We love your grandma.

00:23:40.420 --> 00:23:42.510
We know we could never be your grandma,

00:23:42.510 --> 00:23:44.163
that's why we changed things up,

00:23:44.163 --> 00:23:46.620
TBF.
But if you're like,

00:23:46.620 --> 00:23:48.913
okay, I understand your philosophy,

00:23:50.339 --> 00:23:52.390
but I don't want to miss out on anything.

00:23:52.390 --> 00:23:54.810
Well, that's why we created
the last chance section.

00:23:54.810 --> 00:23:55.730
Yeah.

00:23:55.730 --> 00:23:57.160
It's your last chance to go get something

00:23:57.160 --> 00:23:58.298
that's never gonna come back.

00:23:58.298 --> 00:23:59.490
Yeah, and we put it in a section.

00:23:59.490 --> 00:24:00.890
Tell your grandma about it.

00:24:00.890 --> 00:24:02.730
So you can go there.

00:24:02.730 --> 00:24:04.480
Season three.

00:24:04.480 --> 00:24:08.330
Well, January, 2013, episode 13,

00:24:08.330 --> 00:24:10.770
do you remember we tried
something called newsicals?

00:24:10.770 --> 00:24:11.660
Yes.

00:24:11.660 --> 00:24:13.050
Where we would take a story,

00:24:13.050 --> 00:24:16.617
we had taken funny news stories,

00:24:16.617 --> 00:24:19.330
like somebody getting arrested over, like,

00:24:19.330 --> 00:24:21.380
complaining about their
fries at McDonald's,

00:24:21.380 --> 00:24:22.427
and stuff like that,

00:24:22.427 --> 00:24:27.220
and we would use those news
stories just for conversation.

00:24:27.220 --> 00:24:30.360
But then we were like,
let's toy with the format.

00:24:30.360 --> 00:24:32.430
Let's actually sing these things.

00:24:32.430 --> 00:24:34.240
To some royalty-free music?

00:24:34.240 --> 00:24:35.073
Sure.

00:24:35.073 --> 00:24:36.295
It wasn't like, us playing guitar.

00:24:36.295 --> 00:24:41.010
No, so it was a kind of operatic,
talky, singy-type thing.

00:24:41.010 --> 00:24:42.540
Yeah, yeah.
It was fun,

00:24:42.540 --> 00:24:45.990
everybody liked it, but
it didn't really work.

00:24:45.990 --> 00:24:49.890
But it just goes to show you,
I mean, from an early point,

00:24:49.890 --> 00:24:53.530
we were like, trying to develop formats.

00:24:53.530 --> 00:24:56.110
Well, and again, when you look at,

00:24:56.110 --> 00:24:58.960
okay, so episode 13,
"Pizza Thief: The Musical."

00:24:58.960 --> 00:25:02.620
Just get a picture of you
holding a pizza and singing,

00:25:02.620 --> 00:25:04.560
464,000 views.

00:25:04.560 --> 00:25:06.977
The next day, episode 14,

00:25:06.977 --> 00:25:09.160
"Nutella vs Peanut Butter Taste Test,"

00:25:09.160 --> 00:25:10.550
1.8 million views.

00:25:10.550 --> 00:25:12.193
Now again, at the time,

00:25:12.193 --> 00:25:14.970
it wasn't like we were getting
this incredible feedback.

00:25:14.970 --> 00:25:15.803
It wasn't like-

00:25:15.803 --> 00:25:16.820
No.

00:25:16.820 --> 00:25:19.890
You know, this is quite an
operation at this point.

00:25:19.890 --> 00:25:22.120
We do AB testing on titles and thumbnails,

00:25:22.120 --> 00:25:24.530
that's why they change
early in the day, you know?

00:25:24.530 --> 00:25:26.780
And it's become much more of a science,

00:25:26.780 --> 00:25:28.450
at the time, it was more of an art.

00:25:28.450 --> 00:25:31.780
It was throwing things out
onto the internet, and also,

00:25:31.780 --> 00:25:34.640
I would say a week after
that episode goes up,

00:25:34.640 --> 00:25:36.477
we weren't learning like,

00:25:36.477 --> 00:25:37.550
"Hey, people are loving than

00:25:37.550 --> 00:25:39.590
the Nutella versus peanut
butter taste test."

00:25:39.590 --> 00:25:43.160
This view discrepancy is
something that is registering

00:25:43.160 --> 00:25:44.710
over the course of a decade,

00:25:44.710 --> 00:25:47.550
not the course of us then making
decision about our content.

00:25:47.550 --> 00:25:49.687
I mean, I will say six episodes later,

00:25:49.687 --> 00:25:52.700
"Walmart Brand vs Name Brand Taste Test,"

00:25:52.700 --> 00:25:56.270
and then episode 29, "Rhett
and Link Eat Insects,"

00:25:56.270 --> 00:25:57.880
which still hasn't broken a million views,

00:25:57.880 --> 00:25:58.840
which is interesting.

00:25:58.840 --> 00:26:02.010
Which, in the "Walmart Brand
vs Brand Name Taste Test"

00:26:02.010 --> 00:26:04.040
has got almost 7 million views,

00:26:04.040 --> 00:26:04.873
which I, you know,

00:26:04.873 --> 00:26:07.530
and again, we talk about this quite a bit,

00:26:07.530 --> 00:26:09.240
but it just kind of illustrates this thing

00:26:09.240 --> 00:26:10.599
that there's this...

00:26:10.599 --> 00:26:13.270
What we don't do, what we try not to do

00:26:13.270 --> 00:26:15.350
is just follow is just like,

00:26:15.350 --> 00:26:16.290
oh, people like this,

00:26:16.290 --> 00:26:18.969
and so that's the only
thing that we're gonna make.

00:26:18.969 --> 00:26:22.960
So, we still have to do some
stuff that's just for us

00:26:22.960 --> 00:26:25.705
and for the folks who've
been around forever.

00:26:25.705 --> 00:26:26.810
Right.
So, I'm not saying

00:26:26.810 --> 00:26:29.650
we wouldn't do another musical.

00:26:29.650 --> 00:26:32.630
It's just now, 10 years
later there's this mentality

00:26:32.630 --> 00:26:35.609
that like, okay, if we're
gonna do a musical episode,

00:26:35.609 --> 00:26:39.540
we already know it's gonna be
a lot of work, a lot of fun.

00:26:39.540 --> 00:26:41.420
It's gonna be very rewarding for us,

00:26:41.420 --> 00:26:42.771
and the people who watch,

00:26:42.771 --> 00:26:45.200
but it's not gonna make any money,

00:26:45.200 --> 00:26:46.390
and no one's gonna watch it.

00:26:46.390 --> 00:26:48.160
And you just have to be
like, "And that's okay,

00:26:48.160 --> 00:26:49.750
and we're gonna go for it,"

00:26:49.750 --> 00:26:50.583
you know?
Yeah.

00:26:50.583 --> 00:26:52.240
That's what looking back on this is

00:26:52.240 --> 00:26:55.520
sort of just a reminder of
the way this system works.

00:26:55.520 --> 00:26:59.031
And you know, we were still
sharing personal stories.

00:26:59.031 --> 00:27:01.980
I mean, our experiences
of getting acclimated

00:27:01.980 --> 00:27:05.560
with living full-time in Los Angeles,

00:27:05.560 --> 00:27:07.283
having moved across country,

00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:10.885
our kids, our wives,

00:27:10.885 --> 00:27:14.350
making the transition, we
would share funny stories.

00:27:14.350 --> 00:27:16.100
But even over the course of season two,

00:27:16.100 --> 00:27:18.042
that started to diminish more,

00:27:18.042 --> 00:27:18.875
because-
We're running out of stuff.

00:27:18.875 --> 00:27:20.440
'Cause we know that like,

00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:23.080
our personal stories couldn't support

00:27:23.080 --> 00:27:24.670
an entire show forever.

00:27:24.670 --> 00:27:25.503
Yeah.

00:27:28.120 --> 00:27:29.360
Right.

00:27:29.360 --> 00:27:31.740
It's not like we're now
doing that on another show

00:27:31.740 --> 00:27:32.670
that's even longer.

00:27:32.670 --> 00:27:35.850
Episode 31, "Rhett and Link are Moving,"

00:27:35.850 --> 00:27:38.547
episode 32, "New Studio Tour."

00:27:38.547 --> 00:27:43.547
So by that point, the beginning
of season three in 2013

00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:47.223
is when we moved into the new studio.

00:27:48.600 --> 00:27:51.930
We knew at that point we were
making "The Mythical Show",

00:27:51.930 --> 00:27:54.960
because that's what gave us the confidence

00:27:54.960 --> 00:27:59.190
to start renting a space, and
the necessity to rent a space,

00:27:59.190 --> 00:28:00.810
'cause we were hiring people.

00:28:00.810 --> 00:28:04.193
Hired Stevie, we hired Ben, we hired Jen,

00:28:06.072 --> 00:28:08.883
and we moved into that new studio,

00:28:10.360 --> 00:28:12.600
episode 32, and we kept ma...

00:28:12.600 --> 00:28:14.900
and then we had a makeshift,

00:28:14.900 --> 00:28:16.360
they were building the set downstairs,

00:28:16.360 --> 00:28:19.170
which ultimately became the
"Good Mythical Morning" set,

00:28:19.170 --> 00:28:22.200
but it was built as
"The Mythical Show" set.

00:28:22.200 --> 00:28:23.435
And then upstairs,

00:28:23.435 --> 00:28:27.900
we would just set up the card
table, and working with Jason,

00:28:27.900 --> 00:28:29.640
we would still make
"Good Mythical Morning"

00:28:29.640 --> 00:28:31.207
while everybody that we were hiring

00:28:31.207 --> 00:28:33.430
was working towards "The Mythical Show".

00:28:33.430 --> 00:28:35.080
Was that the whole season

00:28:35.989 --> 00:28:37.007
in that space,

00:28:37.007 --> 00:28:39.880
was all of season three
in that upstairs space

00:28:39.880 --> 00:28:40.810
at the old studio?

00:28:40.810 --> 00:28:43.760
From episode 32 to episode 60.

00:28:43.760 --> 00:28:47.070
So, and when we were
up there in that space,

00:28:47.070 --> 00:28:50.790
our energy started to be diverted

00:28:50.790 --> 00:28:54.670
into preparating for "The Mythical Show",

00:28:54.670 --> 00:28:58.500
so it's like we couldn't
prepare as much for an episode.

00:28:58.500 --> 00:29:01.714
So I remember telling Jason,
like this is a problem.

00:29:01.714 --> 00:29:05.120
Episode 35, "Best Movie Theme Songs."

00:29:05.120 --> 00:29:07.830
But instead of us alternating

00:29:07.830 --> 00:29:10.790
and saying what we think are
the best movie theme songs,

00:29:10.790 --> 00:29:13.200
we turned it into a game.

00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:16.370
I'm not saying this is the
first game we ever played,

00:29:16.370 --> 00:29:18.480
but it could have been,

00:29:18.480 --> 00:29:21.220
but it was in this era that
we started to game-ify things

00:29:21.220 --> 00:29:24.360
because it took the pressure
off of us to prepare a lot

00:29:24.360 --> 00:29:26.059
when we had to devote that type of energy

00:29:26.059 --> 00:29:27.780
to "The Mythical Show".

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:28.990
Yeah, that's a good point.

00:29:28.990 --> 00:29:32.050
Episode 46, "Most Amazing
Optical Illusions"

00:29:32.050 --> 00:29:34.040
got 22 million views,

00:29:34.040 --> 00:29:37.640
but episode 55 and 56 were both games,

00:29:37.640 --> 00:29:42.320
cartoon theme songs games,
greatest April fool's game,

00:29:42.320 --> 00:29:44.330
you know, where one of
us will quiz the other,

00:29:44.330 --> 00:29:45.460
or Jason would quiz both of us.

00:29:45.460 --> 00:29:47.537
And it was, yeah, it was-

00:29:47.537 --> 00:29:48.407
So games kind of came out of this-

00:29:48.407 --> 00:29:50.119
It was an efficiency thing.

00:29:50.119 --> 00:29:51.480
It was like,

00:29:51.480 --> 00:29:52.780
yeah, again,

00:29:52.780 --> 00:29:54.350
we're gonna digest all this information

00:29:54.350 --> 00:29:57.130
and have to spit it back out,
or just perform and react.

00:29:57.130 --> 00:29:59.337
But it wasn't that we were thinking,

00:29:59.337 --> 00:30:00.640
"Games will be fun."

00:30:00.640 --> 00:30:04.230
It was like, no, "We
need something that takes

00:30:04.230 --> 00:30:07.470
the pressure off, games can do that."

00:30:07.470 --> 00:30:11.940
It moves the prep, the
writing, if you will.

00:30:11.940 --> 00:30:14.720
It kind of started to convince us,

00:30:14.720 --> 00:30:16.933
because Jason was an editor,

00:30:16.933 --> 00:30:19.860
and maybe he would have
done some research,

00:30:19.860 --> 00:30:23.110
but we never allowed
ourselves to think that like,

00:30:23.110 --> 00:30:25.979
we could ever hire writers.

00:30:25.979 --> 00:30:26.817
But then you're like,

00:30:26.817 --> 00:30:27.941
"Hey, Jason,

00:30:27.941 --> 00:30:30.366
write this game."
We didn't think of

00:30:30.366 --> 00:30:31.199
the show-
And all of the sudden,

00:30:31.199 --> 00:30:32.250
we have a writer, we just didn't know it.

00:30:32.250 --> 00:30:36.465
We didn't think of the show
as a show that needed writing,

00:30:36.465 --> 00:30:37.860
because when we thought about writing,

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:40.723
we thought about scripted stuff.

00:30:40.723 --> 00:30:41.910
Exactly.

00:30:41.910 --> 00:30:44.520
In fact, that's still
often a misconception

00:30:44.520 --> 00:30:46.365
when people find out
that there's writers for

00:30:46.365 --> 00:30:48.150
"Good Mythical Morning,"

00:30:48.150 --> 00:30:51.750
they interpret that as, "So
what you're saying is scripted?"

00:30:51.750 --> 00:30:54.380
It's just, no, what we're doing,

00:30:54.380 --> 00:30:56.370
what we're learning about,
the game that we're playing,

00:30:56.370 --> 00:30:58.020
the question that was being asked,

00:30:58.020 --> 00:30:58.910
that script.
Yeah,

00:30:58.910 --> 00:31:01.093
the name of the game on the title screen.

00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:03.050
There's so many details,

00:31:03.050 --> 00:31:05.260
there's so much to be written

00:31:05.260 --> 00:31:07.560
when you actually start thinking about it.

00:31:07.560 --> 00:31:08.803
But yeah, as creators, it's like,

00:31:08.803 --> 00:31:12.797
it's one thing to entrust
someone else to edit you,

00:31:12.797 --> 00:31:15.790
that's a journey a lot of
creators get hung up on,

00:31:15.790 --> 00:31:17.580
but then moving to a point
where you can get somebody

00:31:17.580 --> 00:31:19.150
to write for you,

00:31:19.150 --> 00:31:22.347
it's like, oh, you can
think of it differently.

00:31:22.347 --> 00:31:25.730
It's not putting words in
somebody's mouth necessarily.

00:31:25.730 --> 00:31:28.180
So all of that up into
episode 60 was leading

00:31:28.180 --> 00:31:31.871
up to "The Mythical Show",
which was 12 episodes.

00:31:31.871 --> 00:31:35.220
And even now, looking back
at "The Mythical Show,"

00:31:35.220 --> 00:31:39.120
and very, haven't watched
it in a long time,

00:31:39.120 --> 00:31:42.980
but I'm sure I'd be proud of it,

00:31:42.980 --> 00:31:46.030
and I'd be very endeared by it.

00:31:46.030 --> 00:31:48.740
And I'm sure there's a lot of things

00:31:48.740 --> 00:31:52.905
embedded in these half hours
that we carried forward

00:31:52.905 --> 00:31:53.738
creatively.
Oh, yeah.

00:31:53.738 --> 00:31:55.770
We'd would have to sit
down and watch them all,

00:31:55.770 --> 00:31:57.050
I'm sure people could that out.

00:31:57.050 --> 00:31:59.670
But only 2 of the 12 to this day

00:31:59.670 --> 00:32:01.260
have broken a million views.

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:03.230
Again, it's just the longer form thing

00:32:03.230 --> 00:32:05.026
is not what were gone through.
10 of them,

00:32:05.026 --> 00:32:05.859
and, you know,

00:32:05.859 --> 00:32:09.570
that was YouTube giving us a
chunk of money to hire people

00:32:09.570 --> 00:32:12.963
to make this show to
experiment with longer format.

00:32:12.963 --> 00:32:14.713
And then, you know,

00:32:14.713 --> 00:32:18.660
there's parallels between
that and when we did

00:32:18.660 --> 00:32:23.120
the expanded version of GMM
that comes later, GMM 22,

00:32:23.120 --> 00:32:25.640
that like, okay, even
though it didn't work,

00:32:25.640 --> 00:32:27.480
there was an infusion of create,

00:32:27.480 --> 00:32:31.831
like trying so many things
at such an increased rate,

00:32:31.831 --> 00:32:34.690
and figuring out how to work with a team

00:32:34.690 --> 00:32:38.700
that is growing in the
process is something that then

00:32:38.700 --> 00:32:39.950
has ripple effects,

00:32:39.950 --> 00:32:42.090
because we kept Stevie on.

00:32:42.090 --> 00:32:43.504
And then it was like, okay,

00:32:43.504 --> 00:32:45.510
we're going back to
"Good Mythical Morning,"

00:32:45.510 --> 00:32:48.228
and it needs to be part of your focus,

00:32:48.228 --> 00:32:52.210
"The Mythical Show", which
was your focus, is going away.

00:32:52.210 --> 00:32:56.161
But I kinda, I remember the mindset

00:32:56.161 --> 00:33:00.224
going into "The Mythical Show",
at least as I was thinking,

00:33:00.224 --> 00:33:02.944
I was very excited about
"The Mythical Show",

00:33:02.944 --> 00:33:06.870
and there was a big part
of me that was thinking

00:33:06.870 --> 00:33:10.130
if this works, this could be what we do

00:33:10.130 --> 00:33:11.610
instead of "Good Mythical Morning."

00:33:11.610 --> 00:33:12.443
Oh, yeah.

00:33:12.443 --> 00:33:13.276
Right?

00:33:13.276 --> 00:33:16.140
Because again, I was more excited about-

00:33:16.140 --> 00:33:18.050
There was no plan to go back

00:33:18.050 --> 00:33:19.860
to "Good Mythical Morning"
that I can recall.

00:33:19.860 --> 00:33:23.146
I was super excited
about this high level of,

00:33:23.146 --> 00:33:25.210
there's so much creativity just put

00:33:25.210 --> 00:33:27.827
into one particular episode,
and there's a music video,

00:33:27.827 --> 00:33:29.820
and there's a sketch,
and then there's a game.

00:33:29.820 --> 00:33:32.868
So it was a little bit of
everything that we could do,

00:33:32.868 --> 00:33:36.565
but pretty early on, it
became clear that like, wow,

00:33:36.565 --> 00:33:39.860
again, there's this weird
thing that the feedback

00:33:39.860 --> 00:33:40.923
that we're getting,

00:33:40.923 --> 00:33:44.045
and I'm not talking about
the quality of the feedback,

00:33:44.045 --> 00:33:45.500
I'm talking about the
quantity of the feedback.

00:33:45.500 --> 00:33:50.500
Like, some people who love what we do,

00:33:50.770 --> 00:33:53.070
and love all the creativity are like,

00:33:53.070 --> 00:33:55.170
so excited about something
like "The Mythical Show",

00:33:55.170 --> 00:33:57.200
but you can't like, we couldn't have kept,

00:33:57.200 --> 00:33:58.740
like we couldn't have, first of all,

00:33:58.740 --> 00:34:02.080
YouTube wasn't gonna give
us any more money for that.

00:34:02.080 --> 00:34:04.720
But even the money they
gave us to do that show

00:34:04.720 --> 00:34:06.790
wasn't sustainable, like the
business would have just like,

00:34:06.790 --> 00:34:08.937
completely gone under if we were like,

00:34:08.937 --> 00:34:11.450
"Let's just keep making
that show with that money

00:34:11.450 --> 00:34:13.000
that we're getting from YouTube."

00:34:13.000 --> 00:34:15.240
Like, it wasn't a
sustainable business model,

00:34:15.240 --> 00:34:17.420
but there was a little bit of this,

00:34:17.420 --> 00:34:19.550
ah, man, we put this thing out there

00:34:19.550 --> 00:34:24.460
that felt like it was
creatively innovative and oh,

00:34:24.460 --> 00:34:27.730
okay, well I guess we'll go
back to talking to each other

00:34:27.730 --> 00:34:29.460
at a desk every single day.

00:34:29.460 --> 00:34:32.340
To me there was a little
bit of like, ah, really?

00:34:32.340 --> 00:34:33.620
Okay, okay.

00:34:33.620 --> 00:34:34.630
This is what you want from us?

00:34:34.630 --> 00:34:39.550
You don't want us to do this
incredibly creative stuff?

00:34:39.550 --> 00:34:42.240
But also the thing that
was happening, and listen,

00:34:42.240 --> 00:34:43.550
I don't want to...

00:34:43.550 --> 00:34:45.760
My mind has shifted a lot
over the years on this,

00:34:45.760 --> 00:34:50.050
because I didn't appreciate
at this stage eight years ago.

00:34:50.050 --> 00:34:53.970
I still had not accepted
the fact that this medium

00:34:54.930 --> 00:34:57.680
was very different than traditional media

00:34:57.680 --> 00:35:01.960
in that it was much more
about connecting with people.

00:35:01.960 --> 00:35:05.560
And one of the things that
people were appreciating

00:35:05.560 --> 00:35:07.450
was not that we could write a great song

00:35:07.450 --> 00:35:10.958
or do something funny as a sketch,

00:35:10.958 --> 00:35:12.247
it was that they're like,

00:35:12.247 --> 00:35:14.520
"I like being with those guys,

00:35:14.520 --> 00:35:15.990
and being a part of their friendship,

00:35:15.990 --> 00:35:18.740
and seeing them crack each other
up, and seeing them laugh."

00:35:18.740 --> 00:35:19.573
Yeah.

00:35:19.573 --> 00:35:22.100
That was the experience
that was comforting people

00:35:22.100 --> 00:35:23.550
and entertaining people.

00:35:23.550 --> 00:35:25.490
And it's just a mind shift.

00:35:25.490 --> 00:35:26.963
It doesn't mean that we don't

00:35:26.963 --> 00:35:29.030
have all these creative aspirations,

00:35:29.030 --> 00:35:30.180
it's just now we kind of understand

00:35:30.180 --> 00:35:31.607
that there's a certain place for those

00:35:31.607 --> 00:35:35.310
that's probably not gonna
be a half hour YouTube video

00:35:35.310 --> 00:35:38.030
on our "Good Mythical Morning" channel.

00:35:38.030 --> 00:35:40.340
But that was the first sort of like,

00:35:40.340 --> 00:35:41.712
throwing this thing out there

00:35:41.712 --> 00:35:43.840
thinking that maybe this
will change everything,

00:35:43.840 --> 00:35:45.870
maybe we'll have the
first ever really popular

00:35:45.870 --> 00:35:47.360
half-hour comedy show.

00:35:47.360 --> 00:35:49.290
It'll be like "The Carol Burnett Show,"

00:35:49.290 --> 00:35:51.860
but it'll be on YouTube,
and it'll go for 10 years.

00:35:51.860 --> 00:35:53.190
No, that's not what happened.

00:35:53.190 --> 00:35:56.121
So we went back in fall of 2013,

00:35:56.121 --> 00:36:00.679
leading up to season four
of "Good Mythical Morning."

00:36:00.679 --> 00:36:04.143
We made that weekend sucks ad, you know?

00:36:05.070 --> 00:36:06.930
When "Good Mythical
Morning" is not around,

00:36:06.930 --> 00:36:08.690
the weekend sucked.

00:36:08.690 --> 00:36:13.090
Episode one, which is the
275th episode overall,

00:36:13.090 --> 00:36:15.593
we were on the new set, we had altered

00:36:15.593 --> 00:36:18.010
"The Mythical Show" set to be

00:36:18.010 --> 00:36:20.280
the "Good Mythical Morning" set.

00:36:20.280 --> 00:36:22.310
We started saying let's talk about that,

00:36:22.310 --> 00:36:27.310
and we moved the fan
videos to be for the wheel,

00:36:28.240 --> 00:36:29.310
spin the wheel.

00:36:29.310 --> 00:36:33.960
That was all part of a
really focused evaluation

00:36:33.960 --> 00:36:36.690
that involves Stevie, of like, okay,

00:36:36.690 --> 00:36:38.420
we're changing the way we're producing it,

00:36:38.420 --> 00:36:39.260
we're applying this team,

00:36:39.260 --> 00:36:42.190
but that was also in Stevie began to,

00:36:42.190 --> 00:36:46.230
she turned her attention
where she had not really been

00:36:47.110 --> 00:36:49.570
involved at all creatively with GMM.

00:36:49.570 --> 00:36:50.630
That after "The Mythical Show",

00:36:50.630 --> 00:36:54.470
she turned her creative
instincts and producer instincts

00:36:54.470 --> 00:36:57.270
onto GMM, and so the
three of us were sort of

00:36:57.270 --> 00:36:58.500
attacking this thing.

00:36:58.500 --> 00:37:01.280
And we had a few writers at the time.

00:37:01.280 --> 00:37:02.113
Yeah, yeah,

00:37:02.113 --> 00:37:05.620
and we kept our writers on board.

00:37:05.620 --> 00:37:06.543
Actually,

00:37:07.830 --> 00:37:08.663
that's not true.

00:37:08.663 --> 00:37:11.640
We didn't have any writers yet besides...

00:37:14.570 --> 00:37:16.407
I'm pretty sure we didn't,

00:37:16.407 --> 00:37:18.340
because this was also when-
I don't remember

00:37:18.340 --> 00:37:19.300
this time.
We started

00:37:19.300 --> 00:37:22.230
Good Mythical More with season three,

00:37:22.230 --> 00:37:23.690
but it was on our website.

00:37:23.690 --> 00:37:24.790
So we were still doing,

00:37:24.790 --> 00:37:27.340
we started at season three, but we had it

00:37:27.340 --> 00:37:28.430
at RhettandLink.com.

00:37:28.430 --> 00:37:31.090
Yep, we were trying to get
people to go to our website,

00:37:31.090 --> 00:37:32.197
and that's the only way you could watch

00:37:32.197 --> 00:37:34.180
"Good Mythical More."

00:37:34.180 --> 00:37:37.610
Episode four was a spicy pepper challenge.

00:37:37.610 --> 00:37:39.210
And Ben, Stevie,

00:37:39.210 --> 00:37:43.359
and Jason and Jen all came on
during "Good Mythical More,"

00:37:43.359 --> 00:37:46.940
and I was like, this is the
whole team bonding experience.

00:37:46.940 --> 00:37:49.900
Where we impromptu brought them on camera

00:37:49.900 --> 00:37:52.510
and made them eat the peppers.

00:37:52.510 --> 00:37:53.343
Yeah.

00:37:53.343 --> 00:37:56.230
And so it was just kinda
like something that happened,

00:37:56.230 --> 00:37:58.430
and that's why I think that
was the extent of our team

00:37:58.430 --> 00:38:00.820
at the beginning of season three.

00:38:00.820 --> 00:38:05.820
Episode 27, "6 Legal Things
That Should Be Illegal."

00:38:06.130 --> 00:38:09.060
So we were doing these
list episodes like, okay,

00:38:09.060 --> 00:38:11.740
if Buzzfeed can do all
these click-baity stuff,

00:38:11.740 --> 00:38:15.938
and there's all these click-baity
listicle titles out there

00:38:15.938 --> 00:38:19.840
all over the internet, we're
basically capitalizing on that.

00:38:19.840 --> 00:38:21.580
Yeah, and it was really working.

00:38:21.580 --> 00:38:24.420
You got 8 million views
for six legal things

00:38:24.420 --> 00:38:26.430
that should be illegal.

00:38:26.430 --> 00:38:28.530
I just can't resist clicking on that.

00:38:28.530 --> 00:38:31.580
It's like, no one gives
a shit about that now.

00:38:31.580 --> 00:38:32.413
It's like, you know?

00:38:32.413 --> 00:38:33.357
It's so-
Yeah.

00:38:33.357 --> 00:38:35.115
But I mean, we learned that over time,

00:38:35.115 --> 00:38:36.980
that's why those lists went away.

00:38:36.980 --> 00:38:38.597
Everyone learned every list.

00:38:38.597 --> 00:38:39.743
Yeah, it's like

00:38:39.743 --> 00:38:40.967
they're tired of this.
Everyone was like,

00:38:40.967 --> 00:38:41.890
"I've been through every list,

00:38:41.890 --> 00:38:42.860
there's no more list."

00:38:42.860 --> 00:38:44.520
Yeah, they were clicked baited so much,

00:38:44.520 --> 00:38:45.709
there's only, you know,

00:38:45.709 --> 00:38:48.240
I was burned one too many times

00:38:48.240 --> 00:38:49.537
with a list that didn't come through.

00:38:49.537 --> 00:38:51.509
And the thumbnail strategy at this point,

00:38:51.509 --> 00:38:53.960
I'm noticing, with that episode,

00:38:53.960 --> 00:38:55.650
it just has like a stock photo

00:38:55.650 --> 00:38:58.850
of a woman sneezing into blackness.

00:38:58.850 --> 00:39:01.296
That's a a good thumbnail.
What I don't see

00:39:01.296 --> 00:39:03.780
is I don't see your face going.

00:39:03.780 --> 00:39:04.613
No.

00:39:04.613 --> 00:39:05.540
Or my face going.

00:39:05.540 --> 00:39:06.373
No.

00:39:06.373 --> 00:39:08.000
I don't see that.

00:39:08.000 --> 00:39:09.010
Yeah, it was like this.

00:39:09.010 --> 00:39:12.450
I remember I would work closely
with Jason to figure out

00:39:12.450 --> 00:39:15.510
some sort of eye-catching
Photoshop thing that was like,

00:39:15.510 --> 00:39:17.320
okay, here's a sneeze.

00:39:17.320 --> 00:39:19.590
There was a completely isolated,

00:39:19.590 --> 00:39:23.720
self-contained strategy
for every single thumbnail,

00:39:23.720 --> 00:39:26.100
as opposed to a thumbnail strategy,

00:39:26.100 --> 00:39:27.660
which is-
That got exhausting.

00:39:27.660 --> 00:39:30.120
That's a function of
what YouTube has changed.

00:39:30.120 --> 00:39:33.920
We also started "Ear
Biscuits," episode 25.

00:39:33.920 --> 00:39:36.800
We announced "Ear
Biscuits" on season four,

00:39:36.800 --> 00:39:40.080
and we started doing Saturday
"Ear Biscuit" promos.

00:39:40.080 --> 00:39:42.660
We'd put a video up on Saturday
where we would talk about

00:39:42.660 --> 00:39:43.493
the "Ear Biscuit,"

00:39:43.493 --> 00:39:46.340
because there was a video,
and we would tell a story.

00:39:46.340 --> 00:39:50.370
But it wasn't a clip, it was
related to the "Ear Biscuit"

00:39:50.370 --> 00:39:51.470
that you could go and listen to.

00:39:51.470 --> 00:39:52.890
Yeah, so like we taught-

00:39:52.890 --> 00:39:54.090
I forgot all about this.

00:39:54.090 --> 00:39:54.923
Like, yeah.

00:39:54.923 --> 00:39:58.327
In October we had Di Franco on the show,

00:39:58.327 --> 00:39:59.900
and we were talking about that,

00:39:59.900 --> 00:40:01.970
and he talked about a friend of his.

00:40:01.970 --> 00:40:04.520
And so then on our Sunday episode,

00:40:04.520 --> 00:40:09.520
we spend a few minutes talking
about Ben, back then in 2013,

00:40:09.590 --> 00:40:11.770
you know what ended up
being an entire episode

00:40:11.770 --> 00:40:14.425
that like is the most
viewed on the "Ear Biscuits"

00:40:14.425 --> 00:40:16.993
YouTube channel.
Yeah, by a long shot.

00:40:18.070 --> 00:40:18.903
Yeah, I just,

00:40:18.903 --> 00:40:20.583
I totally forgot that we did that.

00:40:21.580 --> 00:40:24.781
Episode 36 is when we did the hashtag war.

00:40:24.781 --> 00:40:26.010
Remember hashtag war?
Still one of

00:40:26.010 --> 00:40:27.000
my favorite things.

00:40:27.000 --> 00:40:29.936
Hashtag GMM, we took it
from good morning Memphis

00:40:29.936 --> 00:40:30.769
and good-

00:40:30.769 --> 00:40:31.760
Good Morning, Maryland.

00:40:31.760 --> 00:40:32.600
Good Morning, Maryland.

00:40:32.600 --> 00:40:34.730
Two morning shows, like
two morning news shows

00:40:34.730 --> 00:40:37.110
that were using the hashtag GMM,

00:40:37.110 --> 00:40:40.930
and we basically did a
full on coordinated assault

00:40:40.930 --> 00:40:44.800
to try to claim GMM for ourselves,

00:40:44.800 --> 00:40:46.810
but it was obviously a publicity stunt.

00:40:46.810 --> 00:40:48.600
And we did end up getting featured,

00:40:48.600 --> 00:40:49.930
at least on Good Morning Memphis.

00:40:49.930 --> 00:40:52.090
I think that good morning
Maryland at the time, like-

00:40:52.090 --> 00:40:53.110
Somebody didn't follow through.

00:40:53.110 --> 00:40:55.240
They were a little cold towards the idea.

00:40:55.240 --> 00:40:56.730
We made some jingles for them,

00:40:56.730 --> 00:40:57.790
but I don't-
I'm glad

00:40:57.790 --> 00:40:59.070
you brought this up, because
I was actually thinking

00:40:59.070 --> 00:41:01.403
about this this morning,

00:41:01.403 --> 00:41:06.403
that there is a GMM out there
that is really popular now.

00:41:10.320 --> 00:41:12.773
I told you a little bit about this.

00:41:13.810 --> 00:41:15.640
It seems to be more popular than us.

00:41:15.640 --> 00:41:17.140
You're talking about a new hashtag.

00:41:17.140 --> 00:41:19.463
Yeah, if you just put GMM on Twitter.

00:41:21.120 --> 00:41:22.990
Put GMM on Twitter.

00:41:22.990 --> 00:41:24.760
Sounds like such a dad.

00:41:24.760 --> 00:41:27.200
Look, GMM actresses.

00:41:27.200 --> 00:41:29.823
There is a show,

00:41:31.400 --> 00:41:35.250
GMM25Thailand is tweeting about this.

00:41:35.250 --> 00:41:36.660
I don't know where this show is.

00:41:36.660 --> 00:41:39.693
I don't know what country this show is in,

00:41:39.693 --> 00:41:42.240
but it is incredibly popular,

00:41:42.240 --> 00:41:46.907
and there's a huge cast of characters

00:41:46.907 --> 00:41:50.083
that are on this GMM show.

00:41:51.350 --> 00:41:52.990
And.

00:41:52.990 --> 00:41:54.140
We have to do something about this.

00:41:54.140 --> 00:41:57.252
I mean, I don't think we
can take the hashtag back,

00:41:57.252 --> 00:41:59.390
but I feel like a collaboration
is at least in order.

00:41:59.390 --> 00:42:01.960
Again, I have no idea what the show is.

00:42:01.960 --> 00:42:04.000
I don't endorse it.

00:42:04.000 --> 00:42:05.483
I don't know anything about it.

00:42:05.483 --> 00:42:07.440
So just, let me be clear about that,

00:42:07.440 --> 00:42:09.093
but I just assume it's-

00:42:09.093 --> 00:42:10.300
It's not a band?

00:42:10.300 --> 00:42:11.270
No, it's a show.

00:42:11.270 --> 00:42:14.390
GMM actresses, it's a show.

00:42:14.390 --> 00:42:16.570
I'm a hundred percent
sure that it's the show,

00:42:16.570 --> 00:42:21.172
but there's all kinds of
people associated with it.

00:42:21.172 --> 00:42:24.713
I mean, this is an after
party, a GMM after party.

00:42:25.720 --> 00:42:27.030
We weren't there.

00:42:27.030 --> 00:42:27.863
Oh, yeah.

00:42:27.863 --> 00:42:29.210
It has nothing to do with us.

00:42:29.210 --> 00:42:31.420
That must be fixed.

00:42:31.420 --> 00:42:33.230
Like, we need to be involved with.

00:42:33.230 --> 00:42:35.120
So here's a clip from the show right here.

00:42:35.120 --> 00:42:37.970
I swear if GMM won't
give us another do to.

00:42:37.970 --> 00:42:38.803
Oh.

00:42:38.803 --> 00:42:42.951
I still blame time for
Ringoria, not in-game.

00:42:42.951 --> 00:42:45.843
GMM got no manager to drive is it?

00:42:46.870 --> 00:42:47.703
What?

00:42:47.703 --> 00:42:48.536
I don't know.

00:42:48.536 --> 00:42:49.380
We're gonna figure this out.

00:42:49.380 --> 00:42:51.240
We'll figure that out.

00:42:51.240 --> 00:42:52.073
In the meantime,

00:42:52.073 --> 00:42:53.780
I'm gonna continue to my stroll

00:42:53.780 --> 00:42:56.033
down memory lane.
If you know more about GMM,

00:42:56.033 --> 00:42:58.560
the other GMM, and you're a fan,

00:42:58.560 --> 00:42:59.930
#EarBiscuits.

00:42:59.930 --> 00:43:01.873
Ironically, not #GMM.

