01:07:01.130 --> 01:07:03.400
and do Big Mac versus Whopper,

01:07:03.400 --> 01:07:05.060
and that's gonna perform really well,

01:07:05.060 --> 01:07:08.297
and then we're gonna do
this silly invention thing

01:07:08.297 --> 01:07:12.910
where we wear sauna
pants, which we just made,

01:07:12.910 --> 01:07:16.010
and then we're gonna do
this very quick thing

01:07:16.010 --> 01:07:18.260
called censored kissing game,

01:07:18.260 --> 01:07:21.380
which the writers, we really
had to push the writers

01:07:21.380 --> 01:07:24.510
to come up with comedy bits that then

01:07:24.510 --> 01:07:29.510
where we can play fun games,
or quick improv type things.

01:07:30.550 --> 01:07:35.550
So we were reapplying
our efforts and lessons

01:07:35.700 --> 01:07:39.360
from way back with this
is "The Mythical Show",

01:07:39.360 --> 01:07:40.668
which was a half hour and saying,

01:07:40.668 --> 01:07:43.160
well, you know, we're not
gonna make a 30 minute video,

01:07:43.160 --> 01:07:45.080
we're gonna make each of
these internet videos,

01:07:45.080 --> 01:07:47.900
and then there's room for experimentation.

01:07:47.900 --> 01:07:52.400
And because of that, we did learn a lot,

01:07:52.400 --> 01:07:55.832
but we had to do things like
we have to have a teleprompter.

01:07:55.832 --> 01:07:57.170
This was the beginning of that.

01:07:57.170 --> 01:07:58.930
This is when we started
having a teleprompter

01:07:58.930 --> 01:07:59.880
instead of like,

01:07:59.880 --> 01:08:03.010
trying to commit stuff to
memory for just a few seconds,

01:08:03.010 --> 01:08:06.840
and then say it in twice as
much time as it needs to take,

01:08:06.840 --> 01:08:08.660
and declare it with a lot more prep

01:08:08.660 --> 01:08:10.150
than we have time to give to it.

01:08:10.150 --> 01:08:11.620
And to clarify, okay,

01:08:11.620 --> 01:08:13.790
so when we talk about what's scripted

01:08:13.790 --> 01:08:15.760
and what's not scripted on
"Good Mythical Morning,"

01:08:15.760 --> 01:08:18.033
so for a very, very long time,

01:08:19.180 --> 01:08:20.610
maybe not from the very beginning,

01:08:20.610 --> 01:08:22.570
but definitely their early seasons,

01:08:22.570 --> 01:08:25.700
what we had scripted was, okay,

01:08:25.700 --> 01:08:26.960
at the beginning of this thing,

01:08:26.960 --> 01:08:29.780
when the camera turns on and
we say good mythical morning,

01:08:29.780 --> 01:08:32.180
the things that we're going
to say right at the top

01:08:32.180 --> 01:08:33.230
are essentially scripted.

01:08:33.230 --> 01:08:34.840
It started loose, and it was like, okay,

01:08:34.840 --> 01:08:36.260
I've got a little thing I'm gonna say,

01:08:36.260 --> 01:08:37.093
and then you're like,

01:08:37.093 --> 01:08:38.935
okay, here's how I'm gonna react to it.

01:08:38.935 --> 01:08:42.280
Then it slowly evolved
to a place where it was,

01:08:42.280 --> 01:08:44.522
I think literally written down,

01:08:44.522 --> 01:08:45.980
and you would look at it and be like,

01:08:45.980 --> 01:08:48.520
okay, I think I could say that.

01:08:48.520 --> 01:08:49.920
Again, this is just the intro,

01:08:49.920 --> 01:08:52.170
just like the first
minute or so of the show,

01:08:52.170 --> 01:08:55.600
'cause we wanted to
quickly and efficiently

01:08:55.600 --> 01:08:59.070
get into the good stuff, and
set it up in the right way,

01:08:59.070 --> 01:09:01.763
orient people, maybe there
were rules or whatever.

01:09:02.600 --> 01:09:06.060
So yeah, so that was the existing system.

01:09:06.060 --> 01:09:08.050
And then, but what would happen sometimes

01:09:08.050 --> 01:09:09.850
is we would have this sheet of paper,

01:09:09.850 --> 01:09:10.910
and we'd be trying to get it,

01:09:10.910 --> 01:09:14.130
and we'd go through 2,
3, 4 takes sometimes

01:09:14.130 --> 01:09:16.840
just getting the takeoff of the show done.

01:09:16.840 --> 01:09:18.020
Which is very frustrating.

01:09:18.020 --> 01:09:19.781
And it's taking a bunch of time,

01:09:19.781 --> 01:09:22.990
and I think, all along...

01:09:24.240 --> 01:09:26.060
I can't remember who it
was if it was Stevie,

01:09:26.060 --> 01:09:29.180
or whoever it was was like,
what about a teleprompter?

01:09:29.180 --> 01:09:32.910
We were very anti-teleprompter
because if you know

01:09:32.910 --> 01:09:34.740
what it looks like when somebody
is reading a teleprompter,

01:09:34.740 --> 01:09:35.850
you know what it looks like when somebody

01:09:35.850 --> 01:09:36.780
is reading a teleprompter,

01:09:36.780 --> 01:09:37.810
right?
Yeah.

01:09:37.810 --> 01:09:41.180
And then that makes the
show feel less authentic.

01:09:41.180 --> 01:09:44.020
And plus, we were self-conscious about it,

01:09:44.020 --> 01:09:47.610
but we basically knew
we had to figure it out.

01:09:47.610 --> 01:09:50.220
We had to utilize it
for the show openings,

01:09:50.220 --> 01:09:55.120
and so it was just one of
those things that made GMM 22

01:09:55.120 --> 01:09:56.870
feel off at the beginning.

01:09:56.870 --> 01:10:00.300
It wasn't just about the fact
that it was the wrong color,

01:10:00.300 --> 01:10:02.870
and that we're using different cameras.

01:10:02.870 --> 01:10:05.170
There was a lot of
transitions that shouldn't

01:10:05.170 --> 01:10:07.460
have impacted the audience
experience that did,

01:10:07.460 --> 01:10:12.260
because there were so many
things that we were changing

01:10:12.260 --> 01:10:14.460
in our experience behind the scenes.

01:10:14.460 --> 01:10:16.805
So like, using a teleprompter,

01:10:16.805 --> 01:10:20.343
and the specifics of how we were using it.

01:10:22.060 --> 01:10:23.453
But that ended up being a good thing,

01:10:23.453 --> 01:10:27.040
it's like forcing us to
figure out what are our terms

01:10:27.040 --> 01:10:28.280
for using this tool.

01:10:28.280 --> 01:10:30.360
We learned when not to use it,

01:10:30.360 --> 01:10:32.277
'cause I think we overdid
it in certain times.

01:10:32.277 --> 01:10:33.920
Yeah.
And we also

01:10:33.920 --> 01:10:36.210
got better at, you know,

01:10:36.210 --> 01:10:38.270
if you want to look and
you want to follow our eyes

01:10:38.270 --> 01:10:40.410
and get really specific
you, I mean, you probably,

01:10:40.410 --> 01:10:42.327
if you watched a show enough,
you know when it's happening,

01:10:42.327 --> 01:10:44.110
but we got better at it.

01:10:44.110 --> 01:10:46.400
So somebody who's coming into
the show for the first time

01:10:46.400 --> 01:10:47.233
doesn't feel like,

01:10:47.233 --> 01:10:50.060
"I'm walking into this
stiff newscaster situation."

01:10:50.060 --> 01:10:53.280
We also had writers
writing more comedy bits

01:10:53.280 --> 01:10:55.930
that we were figuring out, is this for us?

01:10:55.930 --> 01:10:56.858
Are we...

01:10:56.858 --> 01:10:59.550
Do we want, do we enjoy this?

01:10:59.550 --> 01:11:00.750
Are we good at it?

01:11:00.750 --> 01:11:04.120
Can we take someone else's
joke and say it as our own?

01:11:04.120 --> 01:11:05.445
There was a lot of...

01:11:05.445 --> 01:11:09.210
or work within an improv
bit where it's like,

01:11:09.210 --> 01:11:13.330
we're gonna be sucking
helium and saying...

01:11:14.820 --> 01:11:17.030
and then ranting about things that are,

01:11:17.030 --> 01:11:19.280
again, on a teleprompter.

01:11:19.280 --> 01:11:21.010
And a lot of things didn't work,

01:11:21.010 --> 01:11:21.843
but-
Yeah.

01:11:21.843 --> 01:11:24.210
It was great to have to force ourselves

01:11:24.210 --> 01:11:25.428
to try these things,

01:11:25.428 --> 01:11:28.850
and at least have these segments that were

01:11:28.850 --> 01:11:31.800
like our bread and butter and
that we knew were gonna work.

01:11:32.770 --> 01:11:34.860
We also had guests come in,

01:11:34.860 --> 01:11:39.170
we had a dedicated effort to book guests

01:11:39.170 --> 01:11:42.800
like through traditional means, like...

01:11:42.800 --> 01:11:43.690
A booking agent.

01:11:43.690 --> 01:11:45.320
That's how you get "Weird Al" Yankovic

01:11:45.320 --> 01:11:46.153
to come on the show.

01:11:46.153 --> 01:11:48.120
That's how you get in
touch with Jack Black

01:11:48.120 --> 01:11:51.520
to come on and sing "Silent
Night" for episode 85.

01:11:51.520 --> 01:11:55.690
We also, at the end of
that year, episode 84,

01:11:55.690 --> 01:11:59.670
we made the "Christmas Booty" music video.

01:11:59.670 --> 01:12:01.550
Decided to re-engage then.

01:12:01.550 --> 01:12:04.900
All we had done was announced
buddy system season two,

01:12:04.900 --> 01:12:09.900
none of it came out until
season 13, January of 2018.

01:12:12.261 --> 01:12:14.390
We reduced our number of videos

01:12:14.390 --> 01:12:17.645
from four to three per episode,

01:12:17.645 --> 01:12:22.645
but we were continuing to
do the segmented GMM 22.

01:12:24.520 --> 01:12:26.236
At the end of 2017,

01:12:26.236 --> 01:12:29.077
it was during this season
that we were releasing

01:12:29.077 --> 01:12:31.140
"Buddy System" season two.

01:12:31.140 --> 01:12:33.467
It was all happening at the same time.

01:12:33.467 --> 01:12:35.691
Yeah, it's also when I
started gonna therapy.

01:12:37.720 --> 01:12:39.130
Yeah, that was freaking crazy.

01:12:39.130 --> 01:12:39.963
Well, you know,

01:12:39.963 --> 01:12:43.700
there's a couple of other elements too,

01:12:43.700 --> 01:12:44.573
that this is not the kind of thing

01:12:44.573 --> 01:12:46.890
that we typically think
about or talk about,

01:12:46.890 --> 01:12:49.630
but I'm sure that if we were to talk

01:12:49.630 --> 01:12:51.730
to some of the folks who work more

01:12:51.730 --> 01:12:53.580
on the production side of GMM,

01:12:53.580 --> 01:12:56.180
a lot of the things that we do now

01:12:56.180 --> 01:13:00.300
in terms of the way
thumbnails are created,

01:13:00.300 --> 01:13:02.300
the way that graphics are created,

01:13:02.300 --> 01:13:07.040
the way the editing
workflow, the audio workflow,

01:13:07.040 --> 01:13:10.090
all this back-end stuff
that enables the show

01:13:10.090 --> 01:13:12.070
to happen every single day,

01:13:12.070 --> 01:13:16.220
those efficiencies were
developed during GMM 22.

01:13:16.220 --> 01:13:17.890
That's not something that
we often think about,

01:13:17.890 --> 01:13:21.853
but anything that worked, we kept.

01:13:21.853 --> 01:13:25.680
What didn't work, which
namely was doing three or four

01:13:25.680 --> 01:13:27.226
or five videos every single day

01:13:27.226 --> 01:13:29.852
didn't work on a number of levels,

01:13:29.852 --> 01:13:32.120
but there's so many things
that we took forward.

01:13:32.120 --> 01:13:33.532
Yeah, we learn how to work with guests,

01:13:33.532 --> 01:13:34.893
and so then, well,

01:13:34.893 --> 01:13:37.080
I won't get ahead of myself,

01:13:37.080 --> 01:13:41.953
because that's the second half
of GMM 22 in January, 2018.

01:13:44.260 --> 01:13:48.653
That's episode 12 is when we
did fancy fast food, big Mac.

01:13:49.680 --> 01:13:51.100
So it's like, that is a staple

01:13:51.100 --> 01:13:53.380
of the mythical kitchen channel now.

01:13:53.380 --> 01:13:55.720
We did our first munch madness tournament,

01:13:55.720 --> 01:13:57.210
which we ultimately...

01:13:58.848 --> 01:14:00.340
That was pretty pivotal.

01:14:00.340 --> 01:14:01.173
Yeah.

01:14:01.173 --> 01:14:02.620
Was it a multi-day thing?

01:14:02.620 --> 01:14:03.453
Oh yeah.

01:14:03.453 --> 01:14:06.277
And then episode 116, "Will It Churro?"

01:14:06.277 --> 01:14:09.050
was the last GMM 22,

01:14:09.050 --> 01:14:11.780
but then the things we
continued from there were

01:14:11.780 --> 01:14:13.799
having guests on the show,

01:14:13.799 --> 01:14:16.520
being able to use the
teleprompter for the stuff that

01:14:16.520 --> 01:14:20.170
we just needed to get through,
like how a game works.

01:14:20.170 --> 01:14:21.003
Right.

01:14:21.003 --> 01:14:22.280
And the intros.

01:14:22.280 --> 01:14:24.910
So it was, there was, like you said,

01:14:24.910 --> 01:14:26.660
there was a lot of efficiencies that like,

01:14:26.660 --> 01:14:29.643
when we said okay, in season 14,

01:14:29.643 --> 01:14:32.660
we're going back to one video a day,

01:14:32.660 --> 01:14:34.913
and then good mythical
more, and that's it.

01:14:36.056 --> 01:14:36.889
And it'll

01:14:36.889 --> 01:14:38.522
forever be that way.
We now have this capability.

01:14:38.522 --> 01:14:41.590
We were like, wait, if we
can make three or four times

01:14:41.590 --> 01:14:44.200
this amount of content in an episode,

01:14:44.200 --> 01:14:48.723
then we can shoot things more efficiently.

01:14:49.760 --> 01:14:52.281
We can start shooting
more episodes in a day.

01:14:52.281 --> 01:14:56.520
We can have more time
to work on other things,

01:14:56.520 --> 01:14:58.692
and still keep "Good Mythical Morning,"

01:14:58.692 --> 01:15:01.343
at the caliber we had.
'Cause one of the things

01:15:01.343 --> 01:15:03.690
that was happening with
the book and the tour

01:15:03.690 --> 01:15:04.871
and "Buddy System,"

01:15:04.871 --> 01:15:09.000
which represented things
that we really want to do,

01:15:09.000 --> 01:15:11.260
they become more difficult to enjoy

01:15:11.260 --> 01:15:15.070
when you just can't really
devote time to them, right?

01:15:15.070 --> 01:15:17.540
And then everything begins to feel

01:15:17.540 --> 01:15:19.848
a little bit like a burden, like, okay,

01:15:19.848 --> 01:15:22.470
GMM, when you want to work on this,

01:15:22.470 --> 01:15:24.930
GMM feels like, oh, that
feels like a burden.

01:15:24.930 --> 01:15:28.400
Or it's like when you've
got GMM scheduled to shoot,

01:15:28.400 --> 01:15:29.990
and, "Well, I need to work on this book,"

01:15:29.990 --> 01:15:31.340
that begins to feel like a burden.

01:15:31.340 --> 01:15:32.173
Yeah.

01:15:32.173 --> 01:15:33.543
We knew that wasn't sustainable,
we also knew the thing

01:15:33.543 --> 01:15:37.272
that we didn't want to do
was stop doing anything.

01:15:38.140 --> 01:15:38.973
Right?

01:15:38.973 --> 01:15:40.720
That's the one thing that
we've never really considered

01:15:40.720 --> 01:15:42.430
is stopping doing anything.

01:15:42.430 --> 01:15:43.263
Right.

01:15:43.263 --> 01:15:46.240
It's just finding a more
efficient way to do it.

01:15:46.240 --> 01:15:48.530
But the beautiful thing about this is that

01:15:48.530 --> 01:15:52.697
it solved a lot of problems
for us, but then nobody,

01:15:54.100 --> 01:15:55.760
like, I mean, I'm sure
there's somebody out there

01:15:55.760 --> 01:15:58.060
who's like, "I was disappointed

01:15:58.060 --> 01:15:59.940
when you guys went back
to one episode a day."

01:15:59.940 --> 01:16:02.510
I know that that group of
people exist, and you know what,

01:16:02.510 --> 01:16:03.603
I love you for that.

01:16:04.604 --> 01:16:08.900
But I think the vast
majority of people were happy

01:16:08.900 --> 01:16:12.480
to get their good old
traditional GMM back,

01:16:12.480 --> 01:16:14.247
but we were able to be like,

01:16:14.247 --> 01:16:17.867
"Hey, we can actually write
this thing or do this thing."

01:16:17.867 --> 01:16:19.930
"Oh, no, we didn't do a
buddy system season three,

01:16:19.930 --> 01:16:21.530
and we probably never will, but hey,

01:16:21.530 --> 01:16:22.944
we're trying to do other things."

01:16:22.944 --> 01:16:24.840
And we wouldn't be able to do those

01:16:24.840 --> 01:16:26.360
if we hadn't transitioned back.

01:16:26.360 --> 01:16:31.163
Season 14, episode 13 is
when we did shuffleboard.

01:16:32.600 --> 01:16:34.290
So, you know we had confidence

01:16:34.290 --> 01:16:38.334
to do these bigger set
pieces, like shuffleboard.

01:16:38.334 --> 01:16:41.557
Also that's when we
released the GMM billboards

01:16:41.557 --> 01:16:43.092
with the body paint.

01:16:43.092 --> 01:16:46.310
Well, and another thing that
is beginning to happen here

01:16:46.310 --> 01:16:51.310
pretty consistently, I
would say that it started,

01:16:52.010 --> 01:16:54.322
it started during season,

01:16:54.322 --> 01:16:57.650
it started a little bit during the GMM 22,

01:16:57.650 --> 01:16:59.710
and then it settles into
itself a little bit more

01:16:59.710 --> 01:17:04.690
in season 14 is there's
a thumbnail strategy.

01:17:04.690 --> 01:17:08.770
There is a face, might be
your face, might be my face,

01:17:08.770 --> 01:17:09.884
filling about half the screen,

01:17:09.884 --> 01:17:14.884
there's words on the thumbnail
itself, like big bold words.

01:17:16.030 --> 01:17:19.710
That's happening more
and more often, right?

01:17:19.710 --> 01:17:20.767
And we had it, yep.

01:17:20.767 --> 01:17:25.620
And then we had a long
streak of high performers

01:17:25.620 --> 01:17:29.750
from episode 29 to episode
36, MRE taste tests,

01:17:29.750 --> 01:17:32.170
six and a half million,
leaving things in nail polish,

01:17:32.170 --> 01:17:35.000
six and a half million, president foods.

01:17:35.000 --> 01:17:36.820
You remember Jordan was talking for the-

01:17:36.820 --> 01:17:38.200
Now we're bringing the,

01:17:38.200 --> 01:17:40.660
I think we're trying to
bring that format back.

01:17:40.660 --> 01:17:41.493
Yeah.

01:17:41.493 --> 01:17:44.362
And this was the season
that we launched LTAT.

01:17:44.362 --> 01:17:49.362
So GMM 22 went away, and
then LTAT, we started that.

01:17:50.400 --> 01:17:52.070
Let's keep going into season 15.

01:17:52.070 --> 01:17:53.040
Okay.

01:17:53.040 --> 01:17:54.270
I mean, this is start getting quicker,

01:17:54.270 --> 01:17:55.440
'cause they're getting closer to things

01:17:55.440 --> 01:17:58.370
that are just new news.

01:17:58.370 --> 01:17:59.760
January, 2019,

01:17:59.760 --> 01:18:02.674
we had that new collagey intro,

01:18:02.674 --> 01:18:06.173
and we were like, we're doing
the Rhett and Link live shows

01:18:06.173 --> 01:18:07.800
that we started in London.

01:18:07.800 --> 01:18:09.840
Right.
So it was like the concert.

01:18:09.840 --> 01:18:11.300
Yeah, we got hypnotized.

01:18:11.300 --> 01:18:14.310
Yeah, you talked about
Finland doesn't exist.

01:18:14.310 --> 01:18:16.155
Like, what's my superhero mask?

01:18:16.155 --> 01:18:21.155
Like episode 91 was "Hot
Food Cold Vs Cold Food Hot."

01:18:22.300 --> 01:18:24.110
It's like getting into
some of those things

01:18:24.110 --> 01:18:25.890
that we're still doing now

01:18:25.890 --> 01:18:26.963
in 2019.
Well,

01:18:27.920 --> 01:18:29.840
by the time season 15 rolled around,

01:18:29.840 --> 01:18:34.237
we were definitely getting this
very clear message that was,

01:18:34.237 --> 01:18:35.928
"Hey, when you guys eat weird stuff,

01:18:35.928 --> 01:18:38.486
or when you guys just eat stuff,

01:18:38.486 --> 01:18:41.013
or when you guys talk about food,

01:18:41.013 --> 01:18:43.335
it's gonna get more views."

01:18:43.335 --> 01:18:48.335
And so the game began,
or became finding ways

01:18:49.730 --> 01:18:51.801
to incorporate food into an episode

01:18:51.801 --> 01:18:54.380
while it's still doing
something completely original

01:18:54.380 --> 01:18:56.730
that doesn't feel derivative.

01:18:56.730 --> 01:18:59.140
It's still the name of
the game in a lot of ways,

01:18:59.140 --> 01:19:01.560
but, as you can see, we continue to,

01:19:01.560 --> 01:19:05.740
and as we continue to
continue to try to find things

01:19:05.740 --> 01:19:08.640
that aren't eating food that
people will get excited about.

01:19:08.640 --> 01:19:09.473
Yeah.

01:19:09.473 --> 01:19:12.050
And you know, it's like
once you get into 2019

01:19:12.050 --> 01:19:15.829
where you got 16 million subscribers,

01:19:15.829 --> 01:19:18.390
we're talking about the
lost causes of bleak creek,

01:19:18.390 --> 01:19:23.110
we've got Josh doing a
show on Sundays after LTAT,

01:19:23.110 --> 01:19:27.000
we're launching alternate
universe snacks with wheat fix.

01:19:27.000 --> 01:19:31.933
And then we did the buoys
creek series, episode 1,630.

01:19:33.810 --> 01:19:36.910
And then by the end of that year

01:19:38.220 --> 01:19:42.220
we had done our bleak creek mini tour,

01:19:42.220 --> 01:19:45.040
and that was when we wrapped up LTAT.

01:19:45.040 --> 01:19:49.100
And then we're all the
way to season 17, 2020.

01:19:49.100 --> 01:19:49.933
Oh, dang.

01:19:51.180 --> 01:19:54.260
Launching gut-check, eating
every Ben and Jerry's flavor,

01:19:54.260 --> 01:19:56.760
not knowing that COVID's
right around the corner.

01:19:56.760 --> 01:20:00.780
So then we had the home
split-screen episodes,

01:20:00.780 --> 01:20:03.620
then like slowly ramping
up our ability to produce

01:20:03.620 --> 01:20:08.511
back in the studio to the
point where by episode 1789,

01:20:08.511 --> 01:20:11.650
we've got this huge putt putt course,

01:20:11.650 --> 01:20:14.590
talking about the most
expensive item at 7/11.

01:20:14.590 --> 01:20:17.710
Yeah, so just in bringing us closer to,

01:20:17.710 --> 01:20:19.300
bringing us to last year.

01:20:19.300 --> 01:20:21.310
Yeah, it's funny as

01:20:21.310 --> 01:20:22.400
I think about-
And present.

01:20:22.400 --> 01:20:26.100
You know, okay going from GMM 22,

01:20:26.100 --> 01:20:29.250
well, again, you think about the era's,

01:20:29.250 --> 01:20:34.250
and the early GMM of figuring
a bunch of stuff out,

01:20:34.465 --> 01:20:39.465
then that early middle
era that we talked about

01:20:39.660 --> 01:20:42.010
with the incredible growth
and the incredible engagement.

01:20:42.010 --> 01:20:42.843
Yeah, the takeoff.

01:20:42.843 --> 01:20:44.189
And then the thing that I think

01:20:44.189 --> 01:20:47.740
I wouldn't have been able to process is,

01:20:47.740 --> 01:20:48.880
'cause you could believe,

01:20:48.880 --> 01:20:50.670
like if you could think,

01:20:50.670 --> 01:20:52.567
and I think maybe some people
do think this is just like,

01:20:52.567 --> 01:20:54.960
"Oh, you guys, you really screwed up

01:20:54.960 --> 01:20:57.280
when you did the GMM 22 thing,

01:20:57.280 --> 01:20:59.700
and that's why their videos aren't getting

01:20:59.700 --> 01:21:00.720
20 million views," or whatever.

01:21:00.720 --> 01:21:02.565
But looking at what actually happened

01:21:02.565 --> 01:21:04.562
is there's this incredible growth,

01:21:04.562 --> 01:21:07.604
and then things sort of settled
into a place just because

01:21:07.604 --> 01:21:09.230
that's just what YouTube works,

01:21:09.230 --> 01:21:10.840
there's changes that take place.

01:21:10.840 --> 01:21:13.210
And we kind of settled into something,

01:21:13.210 --> 01:21:15.248
and then when we did GMM 22,

01:21:15.248 --> 01:21:18.920
the views actually were, and
the engagement sort of went up

01:21:18.920 --> 01:21:21.364
just because there were so many videos,

01:21:21.364 --> 01:21:23.650
but we abandoned that for all the reasons

01:21:23.650 --> 01:21:25.417
that we've covered, and those things,

01:21:25.417 --> 01:21:27.920
and if the efficiencies-
It wasn't refining,

01:21:27.920 --> 01:21:29.950
then it moved into a refining phase,

01:21:29.950 --> 01:21:32.510
which that Reddit thread
called a Renaissance,

01:21:32.510 --> 01:21:34.380
where it was, yeah, it
was a reinvigoration

01:21:34.380 --> 01:21:35.858
of what we do best,

01:21:35.858 --> 01:21:40.070
and doubling down on
those things, you know?

01:21:40.070 --> 01:21:45.070
So you might start to
get more homogeneity,

01:21:45.476 --> 01:21:48.430
but to capitalize on the success,

01:21:48.430 --> 01:21:52.720
and that was really working
in that Renaissance phase.

01:21:52.720 --> 01:21:55.090
You know, this is the thing
that we are always up against,

01:21:55.090 --> 01:21:57.200
and I'd like to spend a little bit of time

01:21:57.200 --> 01:21:59.050
just talking about the future of GMM,

01:21:59.050 --> 01:22:02.290
like what are the next 10 years of GMM,

01:22:02.290 --> 01:22:03.650
if that's, you know, I don't know how long

01:22:03.650 --> 01:22:04.750
we're gonna do the show,

01:22:04.750 --> 01:22:07.050
but let's just say that
as we move into the second

01:22:07.050 --> 01:22:08.230
decade of GMM.

01:22:08.230 --> 01:22:09.063
Yeah.

01:22:09.063 --> 01:22:10.820
One of the things that you definitely see,

01:22:10.820 --> 01:22:11.804
and of course, listen,

01:22:11.804 --> 01:22:14.140
we are very in touch

01:22:14.140 --> 01:22:17.100
with what longtime mythical beasts think,

01:22:17.100 --> 01:22:19.030
and all the opinions that float around,

01:22:19.030 --> 01:22:20.450
and we value all that.

01:22:20.450 --> 01:22:22.000
We're making this show for you.

01:22:24.070 --> 01:22:26.630
You know, we are also frustrated

01:22:26.630 --> 01:22:29.274
by some of the changes that
have taken place on YouTube

01:22:29.274 --> 01:22:33.785
to reward that homogeneity,
is that the word?

01:22:33.785 --> 01:22:37.398
Of a product and a predictability,

01:22:37.398 --> 01:22:40.570
and things that have led us to, yeah,

01:22:40.570 --> 01:22:43.062
the thumbnails pretty much feel the same,

01:22:43.062 --> 01:22:45.530
because whenever we divert from that,

01:22:45.530 --> 01:22:46.960
we get less clicks, right?

01:22:46.960 --> 01:22:48.220
And that's something that, you know,

01:22:48.220 --> 01:22:52.210
you look at the most engaging
YouTuber on the platform

01:22:52.210 --> 01:22:53.840
and Mr. Beast and look at his thumbnails,

01:22:53.840 --> 01:22:54.790
well, he's doing the same thing,

01:22:54.790 --> 01:22:55.850
and he's doing something different,

01:22:55.850 --> 01:22:57.520
but he's doing it in the same way.

01:22:57.520 --> 01:22:59.650
He knows that that
that's the system, right?

01:22:59.650 --> 01:23:01.530
He's not doing the old strategy,

01:23:01.530 --> 01:23:03.170
which is constantly trying something new.

01:23:03.170 --> 01:23:04.470
He's like, "This is what works,

01:23:04.470 --> 01:23:07.240
I'm gonna keep doing it
for as long as I can."

01:23:07.240 --> 01:23:08.073
Yeah.

01:23:08.073 --> 01:23:10.070
It's interesting when
you're making a video that's

01:23:10.070 --> 01:23:12.700
a show that's coming
out five times a week,

01:23:12.700 --> 01:23:14.247
and we're getting this feedback

01:23:14.247 --> 01:23:16.380
that this is what people are clicking on.

01:23:16.380 --> 01:23:19.393
Obviously they continue to click on things

01:23:19.393 --> 01:23:22.530
that are food related, but...

01:23:23.507 --> 01:23:27.765
we don't just do this
show to just fill a gap

01:23:27.765 --> 01:23:30.243
in our business strategy, right?

01:23:31.569 --> 01:23:34.270
Every day we think about
"Good Mythical Morning"

01:23:34.270 --> 01:23:37.300
as this is still our bread and butter.

01:23:37.300 --> 01:23:38.870
Yeah, we want to do other things.

01:23:38.870 --> 01:23:40.150
We are doing other things,

01:23:40.150 --> 01:23:42.260
but this is still our bread and butter.

01:23:42.260 --> 01:23:45.615
This is still something
that people depend on

01:23:45.615 --> 01:23:48.350
in a lot of ways, and enjoy.

01:23:48.350 --> 01:23:50.210
It's not just some fluffy product

01:23:50.210 --> 01:23:51.862
that we want people to just, you know?

01:23:51.862 --> 01:23:53.710
It's meaningful in people's lives.

01:23:53.710 --> 01:23:54.825
It's meaningful in our lives.

01:23:54.825 --> 01:23:57.380
But the thing that we've
been talking about recently

01:23:57.380 --> 01:24:01.380
is as we find ourselves in this platform

01:24:01.380 --> 01:24:06.161
where consistency and
predictability and homogeneity

01:24:06.161 --> 01:24:08.808
are rewarded, right?

01:24:08.808 --> 01:24:13.060
The more you can kind of center
on a strategy on a channel

01:24:13.060 --> 01:24:15.650
and be like, this is
the very specific thing

01:24:15.650 --> 01:24:17.830
that you're getting from this channel,

01:24:17.830 --> 01:24:19.760
and this is the very specific
thing that you're getting

01:24:19.760 --> 01:24:20.854
from this channel.

01:24:20.854 --> 01:24:23.740
We've had this sort of existential crisis

01:24:23.740 --> 01:24:25.760
that we've been dealing with, which is,

01:24:25.760 --> 01:24:27.840
well, we're trying to do a variety show.

01:24:27.840 --> 01:24:31.749
We're trying to do a show that
comes out every single day,

01:24:31.749 --> 01:24:34.400
and covers a lot of different ground,

01:24:34.400 --> 01:24:37.460
but we're doing it in a
place where specificity

01:24:37.460 --> 01:24:40.248
and focus has been rewarded more and more.

01:24:40.248 --> 01:24:41.113
You know, when people try a
bunch of different things,

01:24:41.113 --> 01:24:43.540
they're not necessarily getting rewarded.

01:24:43.540 --> 01:24:44.455
They tried a bunch of...

01:24:44.455 --> 01:24:46.800
we were actually, we
were watching some TikTok

01:24:46.800 --> 01:24:48.130
videos last night, as a matter of fact.

01:24:48.130 --> 01:24:50.030
I'm talking about this
phenomenon on TikTok

01:24:50.030 --> 01:24:52.030
where you can look at
somebody's channel and be like,

01:24:52.030 --> 01:24:53.030
oh, this is this guy.

01:24:53.030 --> 01:24:53.863
And he was trying,

01:24:53.863 --> 01:24:55.460
he's doing like seven
different characters.

01:24:55.460 --> 01:24:58.000
And all of a sudden, something
clicked with one character,

01:24:58.000 --> 01:25:00.150
and then he finds him as that character.

01:25:00.150 --> 01:25:02.430
Now, every single video is that guy.

01:25:02.430 --> 01:25:04.410
And my question was,

01:25:04.410 --> 01:25:06.810
are we moving into a place on the internet

01:25:06.810 --> 01:25:08.240
when it comes to internet content

01:25:08.240 --> 01:25:13.240
where everyone just zeros
in on the particular thing

01:25:14.470 --> 01:25:16.687
that is working, and then it's like,

01:25:16.687 --> 01:25:18.130
you've got this,

01:25:19.250 --> 01:25:20.740
forgive the analogy,

01:25:20.740 --> 01:25:24.400
you've got this cow that's got udders,

01:25:24.400 --> 01:25:25.347
and then you're like,

01:25:25.347 --> 01:25:28.360
"Oh, everything's coming out of this teat.

01:25:28.360 --> 01:25:30.250
Let's just focus on this teat."

01:25:30.250 --> 01:25:32.450
And you just, and this is
what the internet does.

01:25:32.450 --> 01:25:35.400
The internet sends you
to a particular teat,

01:25:35.400 --> 01:25:37.500
and you keep going back to that teat.

01:25:37.500 --> 01:25:38.773
Sucks the life out of a teat,

01:25:38.773 --> 01:25:41.060
and people suck that teat clean off.

01:25:41.060 --> 01:25:44.170
And what we are committed to doing,

01:25:44.170 --> 01:25:47.240
both as a company, as a brand,

01:25:47.240 --> 01:25:49.870
with our whole suite of content
and everything we're doing,

01:25:49.870 --> 01:25:53.960
and including GMM is we want you to suck

01:25:53.960 --> 01:25:56.000
on every teat, you know?

01:25:56.000 --> 01:25:57.940
We don't want to just suck, we
don't want to just give you,

01:25:57.940 --> 01:25:59.900
we don't want to just keep
putting one teat out there

01:25:59.900 --> 01:26:01.920
for you to suck on.
It sounds embarrassing,

01:26:01.920 --> 01:26:03.060
but I have to agree.

01:26:03.060 --> 01:26:04.230
We've got lots of teats,

01:26:04.230 --> 01:26:07.138
and we want you to suck on
all of them to equal degrees.

01:26:07.138 --> 01:26:09.140
I think there's a focus-

01:26:09.140 --> 01:26:10.360
When I think about the future,

01:26:10.360 --> 01:26:13.160
I'm just thinking about
continuing to make sure the udder

01:26:13.160 --> 01:26:17.060
has got lots of teats, and
it's not just a one teat.

01:26:17.060 --> 01:26:21.460
We have a loyal viewership
of mythical beasts

01:26:21.460 --> 01:26:23.520
that are gonna watch no matter what.

01:26:23.520 --> 01:26:25.170
And what we're realizing is that

01:26:26.030 --> 01:26:29.690
this presents an opportunity for us

01:26:29.690 --> 01:26:32.477
and the team to say,

01:26:32.477 --> 01:26:36.680
"Okay, we are gonna determine
to continue to experiment

01:26:36.680 --> 01:26:39.100
to assert that we are
more than just people

01:26:39.100 --> 01:26:41.372
who shove food in our face."

01:26:41.372 --> 01:26:42.205
Yeah.

01:26:42.205 --> 01:26:44.850
And we need to take risks creatively,

01:26:44.850 --> 01:26:49.313
and we need to try different
things, and continue the story.

01:26:50.210 --> 01:26:51.640
You know, there's ebbs and flows.

01:26:51.640 --> 01:26:53.506
There's like, okay, we tried this thing,

01:26:53.506 --> 01:26:57.733
and then it, maybe you say
the whole thing didn't work.

01:26:57.733 --> 01:26:59.150
It's like, that's not true.

01:26:59.150 --> 01:27:02.320
There's always things that carry
on from everything you try.

01:27:02.320 --> 01:27:03.153
Yeah.

01:27:03.153 --> 01:27:05.520
And yeah, even the things
that don't work lead,

01:27:05.520 --> 01:27:09.730
they help direct you into the future.

01:27:09.730 --> 01:27:11.580
So, if you know we're gonna
run interference on this,

01:27:11.580 --> 01:27:16.580
but I think that's where
we're at with GMM is saying,

01:27:17.130 --> 01:27:22.130
okay, how does it continue
to evolve, you know?

01:27:22.152 --> 01:27:27.140
And how do we earmark a certain
number of episodes where

01:27:27.140 --> 01:27:31.257
we're gonna continue to push
innovation and experimentation

01:27:31.257 --> 01:27:34.636
within the parameters of us being able to

01:27:34.636 --> 01:27:39.636
show up and do it within
the construct of the show.

01:27:39.834 --> 01:27:41.690
Yeah, and this has been, you know,

01:27:41.690 --> 01:27:44.420
frankly, with the pandemic...

01:27:46.390 --> 01:27:50.500
we've had a great, great
time during the pandemic

01:27:50.500 --> 01:27:51.551
as a company.

01:27:51.551 --> 01:27:55.670
Our team has been incredible
being able to produce the show

01:27:55.670 --> 01:27:57.294
that we've been able to produce.

01:27:57.294 --> 01:27:59.753
It's just phenomenal what
we've been able to do,

01:28:00.810 --> 01:28:02.120
but it's been tough.

01:28:02.120 --> 01:28:03.250
It's been tough on our team,

01:28:03.250 --> 01:28:05.520
it's been tough on our writers,

01:28:05.520 --> 01:28:08.550
especially who are isolated, you know,

01:28:08.550 --> 01:28:11.786
it's like we have a
production crew that comes in,

01:28:11.786 --> 01:28:16.786
but it's still less than what
we had pre-pandemic, right?

01:28:17.062 --> 01:28:21.310
And we don't just bring in
all the writers all the time,

01:28:21.310 --> 01:28:23.980
because it's just unnecessary
risk at this point,

01:28:23.980 --> 01:28:26.460
but believe that we're getting towards

01:28:26.460 --> 01:28:27.520
the end of this thing,

01:28:27.520 --> 01:28:30.430
and we're gonna get back to
whatever our new normal is.

01:28:30.430 --> 01:28:33.360
One of the things that,
I'm just saying this

01:28:33.360 --> 01:28:36.840
because there's a level of
excitement around the show.

01:28:36.840 --> 01:28:37.673
Yeah.

01:28:37.673 --> 01:28:40.400
And I think that as we think about

01:28:40.400 --> 01:28:44.208
not having the restrictions of a pandemic,

01:28:44.208 --> 01:28:47.840
getting our crew back
here in this building,

01:28:47.840 --> 01:28:50.143
not yet, as soon as it's safe.

01:28:51.460 --> 01:28:54.077
I mean, not talking about everybody back,

01:28:54.077 --> 01:28:57.662
and really doubling down.

01:28:57.662 --> 01:28:59.900
I'm just saying this to
say that when I think

01:28:59.900 --> 01:29:01.656
about the future of GMM,

01:29:01.656 --> 01:29:04.610
even already this year, like me and you

01:29:04.610 --> 01:29:06.060
have been thinking about it

01:29:06.060 --> 01:29:09.060
and been engaging with it in a way that

01:29:10.070 --> 01:29:12.370
because we do so many
other creative things,

01:29:12.370 --> 01:29:13.810
and we have such a great team,

01:29:13.810 --> 01:29:16.540
there are times where I kind of like,

01:29:16.540 --> 01:29:18.516
I don't really have to
think about GMM right now.

01:29:18.516 --> 01:29:21.300
I can just show up and
be a part of the episodes

01:29:21.300 --> 01:29:24.290
in the way that I am, but I
don't have to think about...

01:29:25.603 --> 01:29:28.749
the future of it and like,
where are we going with this?

01:29:28.749 --> 01:29:33.720
But right now there's this focus on,

01:29:33.720 --> 01:29:35.250
this is something special,

01:29:35.250 --> 01:29:38.610
and it's not gonna just fade off slowly

01:29:40.107 --> 01:29:42.930
and be this thing that is
just a relic of the internet.

01:29:42.930 --> 01:29:46.130
Like the focus right now
is like, hey, you know,

01:29:46.130 --> 01:29:49.010
this is incredible that we've
been doing this for a decade

01:29:49.010 --> 01:29:50.880
and there's been ups
and there's been downs.

01:29:50.880 --> 01:29:52.248
We've learned a whole lot,

01:29:52.248 --> 01:29:55.330
but we're not just trying to settle

01:29:55.330 --> 01:29:58.380
into a comfortable thing.

01:29:58.380 --> 01:30:00.320
Like, we're still trying to innovate.

01:30:00.320 --> 01:30:01.470
We still will innovate.

01:30:01.470 --> 01:30:03.698
We're gonna be trying newer,

01:30:03.698 --> 01:30:08.698
bolder things this year than
we did last year, you know?

01:30:09.320 --> 01:30:11.167
We've never been more focused on that.

01:30:11.167 --> 01:30:14.040
And the appreciation that
I have with this process

01:30:14.040 --> 01:30:15.650
that I went through of looking
back through all of this

01:30:15.650 --> 01:30:18.400
is that like, it's a
continuation of what we've done.

01:30:18.400 --> 01:30:21.490
It's like the reason why the
show has gone on so long,

01:30:21.490 --> 01:30:23.500
and will continue to go on,

01:30:23.500 --> 01:30:26.710
is that it's constantly changing.

01:30:26.710 --> 01:30:29.313
It's never just one thing.

01:30:30.751 --> 01:30:34.960
It's most usually changes are not drastic,

01:30:34.960 --> 01:30:37.260
but they're over the course,

01:30:37.260 --> 01:30:38.450
you know, you discover things,

01:30:38.450 --> 01:30:40.680
you realize we discovered things,

01:30:40.680 --> 01:30:42.450
and then you apply those things,

01:30:42.450 --> 01:30:44.208
and so that's gonna continue to happen.

01:30:44.208 --> 01:30:49.208
So I don't anticipate a drastic change

01:30:49.390 --> 01:30:50.620
in "Good Mythical Morning,"

01:30:50.620 --> 01:30:55.070
but I fully expect a continued
evolution of the show,

01:30:55.070 --> 01:30:58.030
and I do sense that we're at a juncture

01:30:58.030 --> 01:31:02.350
where over the course of
this year, it's going to,

01:31:02.350 --> 01:31:06.280
as we spend our time looking
backwards over 10 years,

01:31:06.280 --> 01:31:08.870
we've done our celebration, and now it's,

01:31:08.870 --> 01:31:13.870
all right, we're continuing
to push forward, creatively,

01:31:15.322 --> 01:31:19.380
and so that we can define what the show

01:31:19.380 --> 01:31:21.960
is gonna look like 10 years from now.

01:31:21.960 --> 01:31:22.793
Yeah.

01:31:22.793 --> 01:31:23.626
Over time.

01:31:23.626 --> 01:31:24.737
And like you said, you know,

01:31:24.737 --> 01:31:27.350
and this is actually one of
the things that's given us

01:31:27.350 --> 01:31:31.140
this sort of, I think,
a newfound sense of...

01:31:34.557 --> 01:31:37.870
We tend to, like lots of
people in this business,

01:31:37.870 --> 01:31:39.390
and lots of people in
entertainment in general,

01:31:39.390 --> 01:31:42.215
and probably just lots of
people around the world,

01:31:42.215 --> 01:31:45.500
sometimes you begin to
let a little too much fear

01:31:45.500 --> 01:31:47.660
creep into the way that you
think about things, right?

01:31:47.660 --> 01:31:48.493
Yeah.

01:31:48.493 --> 01:31:50.700
So I think when you've been
doing something like this

01:31:50.700 --> 01:31:51.840
for as long as we have,

01:31:51.840 --> 01:31:54.400
and you've kind of experienced
the level of success

01:31:54.400 --> 01:31:57.970
that we have, and you've
seen ups and downs,

01:31:57.970 --> 01:32:01.440
anytime there's a down, fear creeps in.

01:32:01.440 --> 01:32:02.450
It's like, oh, well, okay,

01:32:02.450 --> 01:32:03.970
what?

01:32:03.970 --> 01:32:06.630
And then fear doesn't lead to innovation.

01:32:06.630 --> 01:32:08.084
A lot of times, a lot of times

01:32:08.084 --> 01:32:12.910
for people fear leads
to taking less risks.

01:32:12.910 --> 01:32:14.407
You get scared and you're like,

01:32:14.407 --> 01:32:15.430
"I don't want to go,

01:32:15.430 --> 01:32:16.570
I don't want to go into the deep end.

01:32:16.570 --> 01:32:17.870
I don't want to try that."

01:32:18.978 --> 01:32:21.150
And we kind of recognize
that, and of course,

01:32:21.150 --> 01:32:22.650
we've had the restrictions
of the pandemic,

01:32:22.650 --> 01:32:27.143
which restricted the ability
to really innovate in big ways.

01:32:28.090 --> 01:32:30.761
But because we have that,

01:32:30.761 --> 01:32:34.210
I don't know what the number
of people specifically is,

01:32:34.210 --> 01:32:37.120
but there are a number
of you out there who,

01:32:37.120 --> 01:32:39.050
like Link said, you
watch every single day,

01:32:39.050 --> 01:32:41.430
like you're just a GMM watcher.

01:32:41.430 --> 01:32:42.880
It's part of your daily routine,

01:32:42.880 --> 01:32:44.850
or maybe you watch it all on Saturday,

01:32:44.850 --> 01:32:48.320
but through the ups and
downs and the changes,

01:32:48.320 --> 01:32:49.940
you've watched the show.

01:32:49.940 --> 01:32:54.465
And you actually give us
the ability to take risks,

01:32:54.465 --> 01:32:58.990
because what we found is,
regardless of what we do,

01:32:58.990 --> 01:33:00.587
there's a certain number
of people who are like,

01:33:00.587 --> 01:33:01.691
"Hey, I'm showing up.

01:33:01.691 --> 01:33:02.524
This is what I do.

01:33:02.524 --> 01:33:03.357
I support you guys."

01:33:03.357 --> 01:33:07.480
Knowing that and embracing
that has really given us

01:33:07.480 --> 01:33:09.782
this confidence to be like,

01:33:09.782 --> 01:33:11.240
man, you really can't,

01:33:11.240 --> 01:33:15.010
we've got nothing to lose by
really getting experimental.

01:33:15.010 --> 01:33:16.945
Yeah, it's a great place to be in

01:33:16.945 --> 01:33:19.230
to have 10 years under our belt,

01:33:19.230 --> 01:33:24.230
and to know that we've
got that loyal connection

01:33:24.616 --> 01:33:27.000
with those mythical beasts,

01:33:27.000 --> 01:33:32.000
and it's extremely comforting,
and it makes this exciting.

01:33:34.010 --> 01:33:35.110
And you know what?

01:33:35.110 --> 01:33:38.070
As much as we talk about clicks and views,

01:33:38.070 --> 01:33:40.870
and the reason we talk
and think about that

01:33:40.870 --> 01:33:43.820
is because we're trying
to support a business here

01:33:43.820 --> 01:33:45.033
with a lot of staff.

01:33:46.760 --> 01:33:50.542
But when we think about GMM,

01:33:50.542 --> 01:33:53.170
I think I speak for both
of us when I say this,

01:33:53.170 --> 01:33:54.745
when we think about GMM,

01:33:54.745 --> 01:33:56.480
and we think about what we're doing,

01:33:56.480 --> 01:33:59.530
and we think about the
person that we're talking to,

01:33:59.530 --> 01:34:01.230
I'm not thinking about that person

01:34:01.230 --> 01:34:03.510
who just clicks on a video
every once in a while.

01:34:03.510 --> 01:34:04.990
I'm thinking about the person who,

01:34:04.990 --> 01:34:06.630
I'm thinking about a mythical beasts.

01:34:06.630 --> 01:34:07.463
Yeah.

01:34:07.463 --> 01:34:08.540
I'm thinking about a mythical beast

01:34:08.540 --> 01:34:10.990
who is coming back, you know?

01:34:10.990 --> 01:34:14.500
And when we continue a dumb bit,

01:34:14.500 --> 01:34:17.820
I don't re-explain the bit
like this is your first time,

01:34:17.820 --> 01:34:20.338
I just take it for granted
that this bit is for you

01:34:20.338 --> 01:34:22.316
who've been watching, you know?

01:34:22.316 --> 01:34:25.260
And if you are watching
for the first time,

01:34:25.260 --> 01:34:27.790
well, you'll catch up,
you'll catch on, you know?

01:34:27.790 --> 01:34:29.200
It's like stepping into a game

01:34:29.200 --> 01:34:30.530
after everybody started playing it.

01:34:30.530 --> 01:34:31.363
You'll figure it out.

01:34:31.363 --> 01:34:33.950
I think I anticipated

01:34:33.950 --> 01:34:37.780
that I would be a lot more
sentimental at celebrating

01:34:37.780 --> 01:34:39.279
10 years of GMM.
Well why don't you cry?

01:34:39.279 --> 01:34:44.279
I think the reason why is
because it's not over, you know?

01:34:45.615 --> 01:34:47.890
We're already looking forward,

01:34:47.890 --> 01:34:51.130
and I don't think we've ever
stopped looking forward.

01:34:51.130 --> 01:34:53.289
So yeah, it's nice to
look back and celebrate

01:34:53.289 --> 01:34:57.460
on GMM over this past week, to do that,

01:34:57.460 --> 01:34:59.060
and it has been special,

01:34:59.060 --> 01:35:02.470
but having this overwhelming sense of,

01:35:02.470 --> 01:35:04.103
I'm not gonna say we're
just getting started,

01:35:04.103 --> 01:35:06.100
because that's cheesy.

01:35:06.100 --> 01:35:06.933
We're not.

01:35:06.933 --> 01:35:08.170
We've done this for 10 years.

01:35:08.170 --> 01:35:09.144
Actually, we're saying that

01:35:09.144 --> 01:35:11.320
at the beginning of every
episode this season.

01:35:11.320 --> 01:35:13.400
We're just getting to started,

01:35:13.400 --> 01:35:14.700
but you know what I'm saying?

01:35:14.700 --> 01:35:15.533
Yeah.

01:35:15.533 --> 01:35:16.366
It ain't over.

01:35:16.366 --> 01:35:17.558
So...

01:35:17.558 --> 01:35:21.590
I'm not prone to get too
sentimental about something that

01:35:21.590 --> 01:35:24.120
I'm in the middle of
and very excited about,

01:35:24.120 --> 01:35:25.479
and as we look forward.

01:35:25.479 --> 01:35:28.240
So, I'll just leave it at that, no tears.

01:35:28.240 --> 01:35:31.337
Yeah, I mean, if the
show ends at some point,

01:35:31.337 --> 01:35:33.740
which for whatever reason it will,

01:35:33.740 --> 01:35:36.410
I mean, there's gonna
be some circumstances

01:35:36.410 --> 01:35:39.952
that lead to it ending at
some point, I'll cry then.

01:35:39.952 --> 01:35:40.785
Shit-right.

01:35:40.785 --> 01:35:41.619
Shit-right.

01:35:41.619 --> 01:35:42.607
Shit.

01:35:42.607 --> 01:35:44.393
You shit-right, man.

01:35:45.370 --> 01:35:46.203
Okay.

01:35:46.203 --> 01:35:47.777
I got a quick rec.

01:35:47.777 --> 01:35:52.180
I've been listening to the audible version

01:35:52.180 --> 01:35:54.670
of the book "Will,"

01:35:54.670 --> 01:35:56.393
Will Smith's autobiography.
Oh, that's cool.

01:35:56.393 --> 01:35:57.423
Seen the cover.

01:35:58.800 --> 01:36:02.070
You know, being a huge fan of...

01:36:02.070 --> 01:36:03.720
Like, being our age,

01:36:03.720 --> 01:36:08.390
like growing up and experiencing
like the DJ Jazzy Jeff

01:36:08.390 --> 01:36:12.400
and the "Fresh Prince"
era as a middle schooler,

01:36:12.400 --> 01:36:14.720
and really loving music documentaries.

01:36:14.720 --> 01:36:17.640
Like, I loved the first third of his,

01:36:17.640 --> 01:36:19.007
book because it was about that.

01:36:19.007 --> 01:36:22.050
And so if you're my age
and you're into that stuff,

01:36:22.050 --> 01:36:24.905
you'll be into hearing the stories behind

01:36:24.905 --> 01:36:28.850
the making of everything that
led to those first two albums.

01:36:28.850 --> 01:36:31.250
And then the stories of him,

01:36:31.250 --> 01:36:32.607
the story of him getting to be

01:36:32.607 --> 01:36:35.070
"The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air."

01:36:35.070 --> 01:36:37.740
There's some shorter
stories out there on YouTube

01:36:37.740 --> 01:36:39.290
that are absolutely amazing,

01:36:39.290 --> 01:36:43.480
but hearing him perform his book,

01:36:43.480 --> 01:36:46.070
he doesn't just read his
book, he performs his book.

01:36:46.070 --> 01:36:49.380
Like, he does impressions of every single,

01:36:49.380 --> 01:36:52.700
of so many people, and
you can tell it's like,

01:36:52.700 --> 01:36:57.340
these are like his grandma,
and Jeff, and Quincy Jones.

01:36:57.340 --> 01:36:58.960
And so it's really great stories.

01:36:58.960 --> 01:37:00.910
And then, I got to say,

01:37:00.910 --> 01:37:04.460
I wasn't a huge fan of
Will Smith, the actor,

01:37:04.460 --> 01:37:07.453
I was just, you know,
more of the musician,

01:37:10.300 --> 01:37:11.133
and "Fresh Prince,"

01:37:11.133 --> 01:37:14.198
but I actually couldn't
have told you that, like,

01:37:14.198 --> 01:37:17.670
yeah, he was pretty much, for a long time,

01:37:17.670 --> 01:37:20.130
the biggest star on the planet, like-

01:37:20.130 --> 01:37:20.963
Yeah,

01:37:20.963 --> 01:37:23.267
It seems very obvious, but like,

01:37:23.267 --> 01:37:26.190
I wasn't a fan of his movies,

01:37:26.190 --> 01:37:28.414
but then seeing that, and like,

01:37:28.414 --> 01:37:33.150
so as a business owner and leader,

01:37:33.150 --> 01:37:36.840
I enjoyed the second half of
the book in terms of like,

01:37:36.840 --> 01:37:38.930
him saying, okay, as an artist,

01:37:38.930 --> 01:37:40.460
this is what I'm going after,

01:37:40.460 --> 01:37:42.287
and this is the team I'm
surrounding myself with,

01:37:42.287 --> 01:37:44.450
and this is how we're gonna go about it.

01:37:44.450 --> 01:37:49.450
And there's also the
partner aspect of like,

01:37:50.950 --> 01:37:54.480
you know, being a husband, being a dad,

01:37:54.480 --> 01:37:55.710
like all that stuff

01:37:55.710 --> 01:37:56.723
was pretty cool too.

01:37:58.470 --> 01:37:59.720
It's pretty inspiring.

01:37:59.720 --> 01:38:01.040
A lot of fun to listen to,

01:38:01.040 --> 01:38:02.370
some really good stories in there.

01:38:02.370 --> 01:38:05.100
So if that floats your boat,

01:38:05.100 --> 01:38:06.427
I highly recommend.
"Will."

01:38:06.427 --> 01:38:09.240
"Will," the Audible book.

01:38:09.240 --> 01:38:10.800
Well, thanks for joining us.

01:38:10.800 --> 01:38:14.060
Thanks for joining us for 10
years, if you're one of those.

01:38:14.060 --> 01:38:15.350
Here's to 10 more.

